window.helpSearch = [{"url": "getting-started.html#getting-started", "page": "Getting started", "title": "Getting started", "text": "Getting started Start with the supplied reactor project to run a complete case and compare one operating change. The installation guide describes the four desktop downloads."}, {"url": "getting-started.html#run-the-supplied-project", "page": "Getting started", "title": "Run the supplied project", "text": "Run the supplied project Download first-reactor.msproject (online) using GitHub's Download raw file button. Downloading needs internet; importing and running the saved project work offline. On the welcome screen, choose Import archived project and import the file into a writable project folder outside the application directory. Run its Baseline case. The first Compiled run may take several minutes. After Succeeded , open General → Requested plots → Outlet composition → Show . Duplicate Baseline , name the copy Lower flow , and change its Program → Inlet flow first ramp target (step 0 ) from 76.2568 to 60 mol/s . Run the copy to preserve the baseline results. Compare the two Outlet composition plots. Use Results… on the project screen to compare recorded quantities and export them to Excel. Continue with the GUI guide , or create a case from your own physical inputs below."}, {"url": "getting-started.html#create-your-own-case", "page": "Getting started", "title": "Create your own case", "text": "Create your own case On the welcome screen, choose Create new project , give it a name, and select a local folder. Choose New Case on the project screen. The case starts as a draft with numerical defaults; it still needs your physical inputs. In Chemistry , select the gas and solid species and the required reactions. In Bed , enter the geometry and complete every material zone. In Program , set the initial feed and outlet pressure, then define timed steps or a repeating program with a suitable horizon. In General , review the solver, sampling, and recorded quantities. Select the reports needed for the plots and Excel analysis you want. Return to the project. When the case is Ready , choose its Run action. After Succeeded , open the case and use Show beside an available plot in General. Use Report to prepare an Excel export. Press F1 in the application for a short explanation of the screen or case tab you are using. The GUI guide provides the detailed workflows."}, {"url": "getting-started.html#keep-comparisons", "page": "Getting started", "title": "Keep comparisons", "text": "Keep comparisons Each case keeps one latest run. To compare a changed case with a result you want to retain, duplicate the case, edit the copy, and run that copy. Results… on the project screen compares successful runs across cases."}, {"url": "landing.html#welcome-screen", "page": "Welcome screen", "title": "Welcome screen", "text": "Welcome screen Start here to create a project or return to work you have already saved. A project keeps your cases, parameter studies, plugins, and report layouts together in one folder."}, {"url": "landing.html#screen-controls", "page": "Welcome screen", "title": "Screen controls", "text": "Screen controls Control What it does Create new project Creates an empty project in a new folder. Open existing project Opens a project folder or its project.json and resumes it in place. Import archived project Imports a .msproject archive into a new folder. Archives contain inputs and layouts; run the imported cases to create results."}, {"url": "landing.html#create-or-import-dialog", "page": "Welcome screen", "title": "Create or import dialog", "text": "Create or import dialog Field or option What it does Project name Names the project and its new folder. Location Absolute path of the parent folder in which to create the project. Project folder Shows the full destination formed from Location and Project name."}, {"url": "landing.html#where-your-work-lives", "page": "Welcome screen", "title": "Where your work lives", "text": "Where your work lives Keep working projects in a local folder outside the application installation. Use project archives when transferring inputs between computers. Creating or importing a project requires a new destination folder. The Help menu is available before you open a project. It includes the full guides, screen references, search, troubleshooting, About, and licences. About MultiSolid shows the version and a diagnostic summary you can copy. Start with Getting started for the complete first-run workflow, or read Projects and navigation ."}, {"url": "project.html#project-screen", "page": "Project screen", "title": "Project screen", "text": "Project screen Use this screen to organise cases, start simulations, and open comparisons. Each case keeps its inputs and one latest run. Double-click a case to open its five editing tabs; use Back to project to return here."}, {"url": "project.html#case-list-and-actions", "page": "Project screen", "title": "Case list and actions", "text": "Case list and actions Field or option What it does Include checkbox Includes this case in Run all; a study group's checkbox controls its cases together. Inputs Shows input readiness, separately from execution status. Latest result Shows the latest execution status, elapsed time when available, and whether results are stale. Duplicate icon Copies the case's inputs and report layout into a new case without results. Delete icon Deletes the case and its latest results after confirmation. On a study, deletes its generated cases too. New Case Creates an incomplete draft with numerical defaults. Complete Chemistry, Bed, Program, and General before running. Import Case Copies a YAML case into this project. Select run.yaml with its referenced files alongside it. New Parameter Study Opens the study-name and baseline-case dialog. The baseline must have succeeded and still match its current ready inputs. Maximum workers Maximum number of simulations running at once. Each case's General tab sets its numerical threads separately. Run all included cases Validates and runs the checked cases; uncheck unfinished cases first. Cancel execution Stops active workers and cancels the remaining queue. Results… Selects successful cases for a comparison workbook. Its selection is separate from Run all inclusion."}, {"url": "project.html#project-and-application-menus", "page": "Project screen", "title": "Project and application menus", "text": "Project and application menus Control What it does Create New Project… Starts a new project in a new folder. Open Existing Project… Opens an existing project in place. Save Project Commits current editor changes; normal edits also save automatically. Close Project Closes the project and returns to the welcome screen after handling pending changes. Open Project Folder Opens the project directory in the system file manager. Reusable definitions… Opens the library of saved bed and operating-program configurations. Export project… Writes a transferable archive of project inputs, definitions, and layouts, without simulation results. Import project archive… Opens a shared archive in a new project folder. Recent projects Opens an available recent project. Plugins Manages the project's species, property, and reaction definitions and their sources. Help Opens the bundled guides, screen references, search, troubleshooting, About, and licences. F1 opens this screen's reference."}, {"url": "project.html#read-the-two-statuses", "page": "Project screen", "title": "Read the two statuses", "text": "Read the two statuses Input readiness tells you whether the case can run: Ready , Underdefined , Invalid , Runtime unavailable , or Needs update . Execution status describes the latest attempt, such as Not run , Running , or Succeeded . Stale means the retained result belongs to earlier inputs or plugin contents."}, {"url": "project.html#run-and-compare", "page": "Project screen", "title": "Run and compare", "text": "Run and compare Check the cases to include, then use Run all included cases . All included cases must pass validation before execution starts. Maximum workers controls simultaneous cases; each case has its own numerical thread setting in General. Cancel execution stops the active workers and cancels queued cases. Starting a run replaces that case's previous results, even if the new attempt fails. Duplicate a case and run the copy when you want to keep a comparison. Results… opens a case-selection dialog for comparing successful runs and exporting an Excel workbook. Its selection is separate from Run all inclusion. The Project menu provides saving, folder access, archives, and reusable definitions; Plugins manages the project's scientific definitions. Read more: Run simulations · Parameter studies · Saving and recovery ."}, {"url": "general.html#general-tab", "page": "General tab", "title": "General tab", "text": "General tab Set how the case runs and which data it records. This tab also opens the predefined plots available from the case's retained run."}, {"url": "general.html#simulation-fields", "page": "General tab", "title": "Simulation fields", "text": "Simulation fields Field or option What it does Time horizon (s) Total simulated duration. Editable for repeating programs; otherwise derived from Program's timed steps. Reporting interval (s) Spacing of saved samples, independent of the solver's internal step sizes. The final horizon is also recorded. Number of cells Number of axial cells used to represent the bed. More cells resolve finer spatial detail and increase the numerical work. Mass scheme Chooses how species concentrations are reconstructed at interior cell faces. Heat scheme Chooses how species enthalpies are reconstructed for convective heat transport. Both scheme selectors offer: Option Meaning upwind1 First-order upstream value. central Average of the two adjacent cell values. linear_upwind2 Second-order upstream linear reconstruction. muscl_minmod Linear reconstruction with a minmod slope limiter. weno3 Third-order weighted reconstruction using local smoothness. weno5 Fifth-order weighted reconstruction using a wider stencil. The nominal order applies where the required stencil and smoothness are available. Check grid and tolerance sensitivity for your particular problem."}, {"url": "general.html#solver-fields", "page": "General tab", "title": "Solver fields", "text": "Solver fields Field or option What it does Backend — Standard Constructs and integrates the model through DAETools. Backend — Compiled Uses DAETools for construction and initial conditions, then generates and integrates a native model. Compatible compiled models are cached. Linear solver Selects the method used to solve the integration system. Unavailable choices explain their missing runtime requirement. Number of threads (0 = environment) Numerical threads within this case. Zero retains environment limits; KLU factorization remains serial. Relative tolerance Relative contribution to integration error control; smaller values ask the solver for tighter local accuracy. Advanced solver settings… Opens the additional integration controls listed below. Cache status Shows compiled-model cache information as execution progresses. A first build can take longer than later cached runs. Linear solver option Meaning and availability SuperLU Sparse direct factorization; Standard and Compiled. KLU Sparse direct factorization with serial factorization; Standard and Compiled. Band Direct factorization using the matrix's band structure; Compiled only. LAPACK (dense) Dense direct factorization; Standard only. AztecOO / ILUT Iterative solver with an ILUT preconditioner; Standard only. AztecOO / Ifpack ILU Iterative solver with an Ifpack incomplete-LU preconditioner; Standard only. AztecOO / ML Iterative solver with an ML multilevel preconditioner; Standard only. SUNDIALS GMRES / Ifpack ILU GMRES iteration with an Ifpack incomplete-LU preconditioner; Standard only."}, {"url": "general.html#advanced-solver-settings", "page": "General tab", "title": "Advanced solver settings", "text": "Advanced solver settings Field or option What it does Suppress algebraic errors Excludes algebraic variables from the local integration error test; their equations still have to be solved. Concentration absolute tolerance (mol/m³) Absolute tolerance for gas and solid concentration states, on a bed-volume basis; particularly relevant near zero concentration. Maximum nonlinear iterations Maximum nonlinear iterations attempted in a solver step. Nonlinear convergence coefficient Sets the nonlinear solver's convergence-test coefficient. Maximum integration order Caps the variable-order backward-differentiation method at an order from 1 to 5. Scale residuals (compiled) Scales equations for numerical conditioning without changing their solutions. Step growth threshold (compiled) Threshold for increasing the integration step size; a smaller value permits smaller proposed increases. Nonlinear refresh interval (compiled) Controls forced Jacobian refresh after repeated nonlinear corrections; zero uses the default nonlinear solver. Vector exponentials (compiled) Enables the vector exponential implementation in generated numerical code. Band reciprocals (compiled) Enables reciprocal-based arithmetic in the compiled Band solver. Available only with Band."}, {"url": "general.html#requested-reports-and-plots", "page": "General tab", "title": "Requested reports and plots", "text": "Requested reports and plots Requested reports chooses what the simulation records. Requested plots chooses predefined plots; Show opens an available plot from the retained run. If results are stale, that plot still belongs to the earlier run. Plots open in a separate window while they are prepared in the background, so you can continue using the application. Closing a loading plot cancels its preparation. Saved plots are reused when they match the retained results. Report option What it records Temperature Inlet, cell-centre, and outlet temperature. Pressure Inlet, cell-centre, and outlet pressure, plus pressure drop. Velocity Superficial gas velocity at cell faces. Gas concentration Gas concentration by species and cell. Gas mole fraction Inlet, cell-centre, and outlet gas composition. Solid concentration Solid concentration by species and cell, per bed volume. Solid mole fraction Derived solid mole fractions by species and cell. Gas flux Inlet molar flow, species fluxes at faces, and total/species outlet flows. Reaction rate Rate of each reaction in each cell. Gas enthalpy flux Species enthalpy fluxes at faces. Heat balance Stored internal energy, cumulative boundary enthalpy transfer, heat loss, and balance error. Mass balance Stored mass, cumulative boundary mass transfer, and balance error. Plot option What it shows Outlet composition Inlet and outlet gas mole fractions over time; requires Gas mole fraction. Outlet conditions Outlet temperature, pressure drop, and total outlet flow; requires Temperature, Pressure, and Gas flux. Axial profiles Temperature and pressure over time and bed position; requires Temperature and Pressure. Average solid composition One mole-fraction curve per solid species, averaged over all bed cells; requires Solid mole fraction. PCA trajectory A two-dimensional trajectory using all selected reports, with points colored by time and Start/End markers; requires at least one report. Average solid composition takes the arithmetic mean of each species' local mole fraction across the uniform bed cells at each reporting time. Cells with no solids contribute zero, matching the Solid mole fraction report. This is a spatial average of local fractions, rather than the fraction of total bed moles. PCA trajectory uses each numeric report output, including every species and cell or face, as separate inputs. Each input is centered and scaled to unit standard deviation across the saved times before fitting a single PCA basis for the run. Time and position coordinates are not inputs. Constant inputs are ignored; reports with more species or spatial points contribute more inputs. The axes show the first two principal-component scores and their percentages of explained variance. Points are connected in reporting-time order. If the data vary in only one direction, PC2 is zero; a constant run sits at the origin. Nonfinite report values prevent PCA rendering instead of silently omitting times. The PCA basis is fitted separately for each run, so axes can differ between runs. Choose the required quantities before running. Editing a Report worksheet later cannot produce data that the simulation did not record. Read more: General settings · Backend and solver choices · Numerical treatment ."}, {"url": "chemistry.html#chemistry-tab", "page": "Chemistry tab", "title": "Chemistry tab", "text": "Chemistry tab Choose the gas species, solid species, and reactions that belong to this case. Definitions can come from the built-in catalogue or the project's plugins."}, {"url": "chemistry.html#species-and-reaction-controls", "page": "Chemistry tab", "title": "Species and reaction controls", "text": "Species and reaction controls Field or option What it does Gas species / Solid species Lists the species definitions included in this case, with their sources. + Add gas species… / + Add solid species… Opens the picker for that phase. Added species start at zero in compositions or solid loadings. + Add reaction families… Makes the chosen reaction families available in the case. Reaction checkbox Includes or excludes that individual reaction from execution. + Species Adds a family's missing required species, with zero initial fractions/loadings. Bindings… Opens a selector for each reaction role so you can choose the matching case component. Each role selector in Bindings Chooses among compatible selected species; useful when several variants describe the same chemical. Available species, families, reactions, and binding roles depend on the selected definitions and installed project plugins. Their names and descriptions appear in the picker and hover details."}, {"url": "chemistry.html#reaction-graph-controls", "page": "Chemistry tab", "title": "Reaction graph controls", "text": "Reaction graph controls Control or symbol What it does or means Click a node Highlights its immediate neighbours and connections; click again or on empty space to clear. Green / copper / neutral nodes Gas species / solid species / reactions. Red nodes Species required by the selected chemistry but missing from the case. Dashed links Catalysts or rate dependencies."}, {"url": "chemistry.html#build-the-chemistry", "page": "Chemistry tab", "title": "Build the chemistry", "text": "Build the chemistry Add the gas and solid species you need. Check each definition's source when several variants describe the same chemical. Use + Add reaction families… to make the required models available. Check the individual reactions that should run. A family's + Species action adds missing components required by its reactions. Inspect Bindings… when a reaction role could refer to more than one selected component. Set gas fractions in Program and solid loadings in Bed . Newly added species start at zero in those inputs. Selecting a family and selecting its reactions are separate steps. Rates from overlapping selected models add together, so remove an unwanted model when replacing it with another."}, {"url": "chemistry.html#inspect-the-reaction-graph", "page": "Chemistry tab", "title": "Inspect the reaction graph", "text": "Inspect the reaction graph Click a node to highlight its neighbours and connections. Click it again, or click empty space, to clear the highlight. Scroll or use + / − to zoom, drag to pan, and use Fit to show the whole graph. Removing a species also removes its entries from compositions and bed zones. Review those tabs after changing the species list. Read more: Chemistry workflow · Plugin guide · Kinetics sources ."}, {"url": "bed.html#bed-tab", "page": "Bed tab", "title": "Bed tab", "text": "Bed tab Describe the reactor geometry, heat exchange, and initial solid distribution. The preview shows the sampled bed profiles used by the model."}, {"url": "bed.html#bed-settings", "page": "Bed tab", "title": "Bed settings", "text": "Bed settings Field or option What it does Radius (m) Internal bed radius; sets cross-sectional area and contributes to bed volume. Length (m) Full axial bed extent. The final zone always ends here. Solid density basis — Bed volume Interprets entered loadings per unit total bed volume. Solid density basis — Solid volume Interprets entered loadings per unit solid volume, using zone voidages to obtain inventories per bed volume. Gas-occupied bed fraction — Interparticle only Includes only the spaces between particles in gas storage. Gas-occupied bed fraction — Interparticle + intraparticle Includes spaces between particles and the pores within them in gas storage. Ambient temperature (K) Temperature outside the bed used for wall heat exchange. Heat transfer (W/m²/K) Overall wall heat-transfer coefficient. Zero disables wall exchange. Heat dispersion (W/m/K) Effective axial heat spreading. Zero disables it; dispersive heat does not cross the inlet or outlet boundaries. Reversible flow Enables signed interior transport when local flow reverses. It does not turn the outlet into a second prescribed feed. Keep positions In the length-change dialog, retains internal zone boundaries at their current positions. Scale proportionally In the length-change dialog, scales zone boundaries with the changed length."}, {"url": "bed.html#fill-the-material-zones", "page": "Bed tab", "title": "Fill the material zones", "text": "Fill the material zones Field or option What it does Concentration units — mol/m³ / kg/m³ Interprets loadings as molar or mass concentration on the selected density basis. Changing units or basis does not convert entered numbers. Start (m) / End (m) Zone boundaries. The first start is fixed at zero and the last end at the bed length. Zones must meet without gaps or overlaps. Interparticle voidage Fraction of the bed volume between particles. Intraparticle voidage Pore fraction within the particles. Particle diameter (m) Particle size used in transport and pressure-drop calculations. Each solid-species column Initial loading of that selected solid in this zone, using the chosen units and basis. Species come from Chemistry. wt% beneath a loading Calculated share of the zone's initial solid mass; it is not a second editable input. Inspect the preview after changing zones or the number of cells in General. The selected solid species come from Chemistry . Bed geometry also affects the conversion between GHSV and molar flow in Program . Read more: Bed settings · Volume and concentration bases · Boundary conditions ."}, {"url": "program.html#program-tab", "page": "Program tab", "title": "Program tab", "text": "Program tab Set the inlet and outlet conditions over time. Enter initial values first, then add timed steps and inspect the preview."}, {"url": "program.html#program-options", "page": "Program tab", "title": "Program options", "text": "Program options Field or option What it does Flow basis — mol/s Enters total inlet molar flow. Switching basis converts existing numerical flow values. Flow basis — GHSV (h⁻¹) Enters gas hourly space velocity using bed volume and reference conditions of 273.15 K and 100,000 Pa. Conversion needs positive bed radius and length. Mode — Feed Combines flow, temperature, and composition in one step list. Mode — Independent channels Gives inlet flow, temperature, and composition separate step lists. Repeat program Repeats channel steps over the horizon set in General. Unchecked, non-empty channels must have matching duration and determine the horizon."}, {"url": "program.html#channels-and-step-fields", "page": "Program tab", "title": "Channels and step fields", "text": "Channels and step fields Field or option What it does Feed Combined inlet flow, temperature, and mole fractions in Feed mode. Inlet flow Total molar flow or GHSV in Independent channels mode. Inlet temperature (K) Inlet gas temperature in Independent channels mode. Inlet composition Gas mole fractions in Independent channels mode. Outlet pressure (Pa) Prescribed outlet pressure, available in both modes. Initial row State at the start of the run, before any timed steps. Step type — Hold Retains the preceding state for the entered duration; no target is required. Step type — Ramp Transitions to the target over the entered duration. Duration (s) Length of this hold or ramp. Target End value for a ramp. For feed/composition, the … button opens the structured editor. Channel duration Shows the sum of the channel's step durations. Focus / Show all Gives one channel the available editing space or restores all channels."}, {"url": "program.html#feed-and-composition-dialog", "page": "Program tab", "title": "Feed and composition dialog", "text": "Feed and composition dialog Field or option What it does Flow (mol/s or h⁻¹) Initial or target inlet flow, in the active flow basis. Temperature (K) Initial or target inlet temperature. Flow / Temperature checkbox on a feed ramp Enables a new target for that part of the feed. Unticked values carry over. Set composition on a feed ramp Enables a target composition; unchecked retains the preceding composition. Each gas-species field Mole fraction of that gas. Use non-negative values whose sum is one. Total Displays the sum of the entered mole fractions and flags an unfinished or incorrect total. Normalize Scales valid non-negative fractions with a positive total to sum to one, preserving their ratios."}, {"url": "program.html#how-the-settings-interact", "page": "Program tab", "title": "How the settings interact", "text": "How the settings interact Switching modes preserves each mode's inlet steps. The first visit to the other mode copies the initial feed values and starts with no inlet steps. A repeated cycle continues from the last cycle's final values; include a return ramp if the cycle should end at its starting conditions. For a constant, non-repeating run, add a hold to define its duration. Check the preview's transitions, durations, and repeated cycles before running. Read more: Program settings · Operating program equations ."}, {"url": "report.html#report-tab", "page": "Report tab", "title": "Report tab", "text": "Report tab Arrange one case's recorded results into an Excel workbook. A report layout controls the export; the General tab controls what the simulation records."}, {"url": "report.html#worksheet-and-column-controls", "page": "Report tab", "title": "Worksheet and column controls", "text": "Worksheet and column controls Field or option What it does Worksheets list Selects the fixed Case information sheet or a user worksheet to edit. Name Renames the selected worksheet. Rows represent Chooses the coordinate that varies down the sheet. Remove its columns before changing the row axis. Row values Selects which coordinates become rows, using the modes below. Add columns… Chooses a recorded quantity and its remaining coordinates. Case information Shows retained inputs, run settings, status, and provenance for the fixed information sheet. Column dictionary Shows the exact quantity, units, and coordinate selections behind exported columns. Preview table Displays up to 20 rows and 12 columns; before a run it can show expected coordinates with empty values. Counts and validation message Shows the full worksheet size or explains why selected data cannot be exported. Save as template… Saves the layout as JSON, without run data or case identity. Apply template… Reviews worksheet sizes and compatibility, then replaces the current layout on Apply. Cancel keeps it. Export workbook… Writes the complete selected data and information sheet to Excel. Export's Cancel stops writing; an existing file is replaced only after success."}, {"url": "report.html#coordinate-and-quantity-options", "page": "Report tab", "title": "Coordinate and quantity options", "text": "Coordinate and quantity options Field or option What it does Time (s) Varies the saved reporting time down rows or chooses a time for each value column. Cell position (m) Selects cell-centre coordinates for cell quantities. Face position (m) Selects cell-boundary coordinates for fluxes and velocity. Gas species / Solid species / Reaction Selects the relevant component or reaction axis when the quantity has it. All Includes all available coordinates. In this case report, All column rules regenerate with input grid changes. First / Last Chooses the first or last coordinate on that axis; it follows the endpoint when coordinates change. Selected values Uses an explicit list of coordinates. Choose values… Opens the coordinate list; Ctrl/Shift selects multiple entries. OK accepts the list; Cancel discards changes. Filter coordinates… Narrows the coordinate list without clearing selections hidden by the filter. Quantity in Add columns Chooses the output variable and shows its units. Options depend on requested reports; quantities without the chosen row axis are disabled. Remaining coordinate selectors Choose each other axis of that quantity, producing one column for each combination."}, {"url": "report.html#quantity-names", "page": "Report tab", "title": "Quantity names", "text": "Quantity names The available quantities depend on the reports selected in General. Names below use the same words as the picker; each option displays its units. Quantity options What they contain Inlet temperature / Temperature / Outlet temperature Temperature at the inlet, each cell centre, or final cell (K). Inlet pressure / Pressure / Outlet pressure / Pressure drop Boundary or cell pressure, or inlet minus outlet pressure (Pa). Velocity Superficial gas velocity at faces (m/s). Gas concentration Gas species inventory per bed volume (mol/m³). Inlet composition / Gas mole fraction / Outlet composition Gas mole fractions at the inlet, cells, or outlet; outlet composition uses fluxes with a zero-flow fallback. Solid concentration Solid species concentration per bed volume (mol/m³ bed). Solid mole fraction Each solid species' share of the local total solid molar concentration. Inlet flow / Outlet flow Total inlet or outlet molar flow (mol/s). Gas flux Species molar flux at faces (mol/(m² s)). Outlet species flow Outlet molar flow of each gas species (mol/s). Reaction rate Reaction rate by reaction and cell (mol/(m³ bed s)). Gas enthalpy flux Species enthalpy flux at faces (J/(m² s)). Heat in total / Heat out total / Heat loss total Cumulative boundary enthalpy transfer and wall heat loss (J). Heat bed total Current stored internal energy in the bed (J). Heat balance error Change in stored energy minus net transferred energy (J). Mass in total / Mass out total Cumulative boundary mass transfer (kg). Mass bed total Current mass stored in the bed (kg). Mass balance error Change in stored mass minus net transferred mass (kg)."}, {"url": "report.html#inputs-and-retained-data", "page": "Report tab", "title": "Inputs and retained data", "text": "Inputs and retained data Cell positions and face positions are different axes. The editing controls follow the current inputs: All columns regenerates when the grid changes, and First/Last follows the endpoint. The preview shows the retained run's values when results exist. Review selections after changing inputs, especially if the retained results are stale. You can prepare a layout before a run, but exporting needs the selected recorded data. Missing quantities or coordinates must be resolved before the affected worksheet can export. A stale result still describes its retained run, not the case's newly edited scientific inputs. A failed run can sometimes provide saved partial samples. These describe saved reporting times, not necessarily the instant of failure. Use Open latest run log to inspect the execution. Layouts save automatically and remain editable for study-generated cases. Save as template… and Apply template… reuse layouts without copying run data. To compare several cases together, return to the project and open Results… . Read more: Export a case to Excel · Project Results ."}, {"url": "results.html#project-results-screen", "page": "Project Results screen", "title": "Project Results screen", "text": "Project Results screen Compare successful case runs and export their recorded values together in an Excel workbook. The selected cases can include independent cases and study cases."}, {"url": "results.html#case-selection-and-navigation", "page": "Project Results screen", "title": "Case selection and navigation", "text": "Case selection and navigation Field or option What it does Select cases… Opens the selector for successful retained runs. Latest run Shows execution status; cases without readable successful results cannot be selected. Select all / Clear all Changes every eligible case, including cases hidden by the filter. Refresh results Reloads retained results after runs or files change."}, {"url": "results.html#workbook-fields-and-options", "page": "Project Results screen", "title": "Workbook fields and options", "text": "Workbook fields and options Field or option What it does Worksheets list Selects the fixed Results information sheet or a user worksheet. Name Renames the selected worksheet. Rows represent Selects the axis down rows: Time, Cell position, Face position, Gas species, Solid species, Reaction, or Case as applicable. Remove columns before changing it. Row values — All Includes the available shared row coordinates. Row values — First / Last Uses the first or last row coordinate; for case-specific selections, endpoints resolve separately for each run. Row values — Selected values Uses explicitly chosen row coordinates. Choose values… / Filter coordinates… Selects particular coordinates with Ctrl/Shift and filters the list. OK accepts; Cancel discards changes. Add columns… — Quantity Chooses a recorded output and shows its units. The quantity list explains each built-in option. Case in Add columns Chooses the cases represented by value columns when Case is not the row axis. Other coordinate selectors Choose times, cell/face positions, species, or reactions as required by the quantity. All/First/Last/Selected values work as above. Split by case Replaces the current sheet with one editable sheet per referenced case, preserving each recorded grid. Results information Shows run identities, study selections, retained inputs, and available balance diagnostics. Column dictionary Shows quantities, units, and exact per-case coordinates behind the exported columns. Preview table Shows up to 20 rows and 12 columns from the selected data. Counts / validation message Shows full sheet size or explains an incompatible or unavailable selection. Save as template… Saves a reusable layout without run data or case identities. Apply template… Previews sheets and maps template case positions to the selected cases in displayed order. Apply replaces the layout; Cancel keeps it. Export workbook… Writes the full workbook and Results information sheet. Cancel stops export; existing files are replaced only after success."}, {"url": "results.html#select-cases-and-arrange-sheets", "page": "Project Results screen", "title": "Select cases and arrange sheets", "text": "Select cases and arrange sheets Select cases… changes the cases available to the workbook. This selection is independent of the project screen's Run all checkboxes. Existing columns keep their case identities; add columns to include newly selected cases. Choose a row axis, then use Add columns… : Time compares histories at selected positions or for outlet quantities. Position compares profiles at selected recorded times. Case compares values across cases. First/Last resolves separately for each run; it does not calculate an average."}, {"url": "results.html#compare-compatible-samples", "page": "Project Results screen", "title": "Compare compatible samples", "text": "Compare compatible samples Shared rows require exactly shared recorded coordinates. If cases use different grids or reporting times, select common coordinates or use Split by case to make separate editable worksheets. Export does not interpolate between samples. Cell positions and face positions remain separate axes. Use Refresh results after runs change. Missing cases or coordinates stay visible for repair. Successful stale results can be exported when their retained data and snapshot remain readable. To export partial data from a failed run, use that case's Report tab instead."}, {"url": "results.html#export-and-reuse-layouts", "page": "Project Results screen", "title": "Export and reuse layouts", "text": "Export and reuse layouts The preview shows up to 20 rows and 12 columns. Export workbook… exports the full selection, with a Results information sheet identifying the runs and coordinates behind the values. Layouts save automatically. Save as template… saves the layout without data or case identities. Apply template… previews compatibility and maps template case positions to your selected cases in their displayed order. Read more: Comparing cases ."}, {"url": "studies.html#parameter-study-screen", "page": "Parameter study screen", "title": "Parameter study screen", "text": "Parameter study screen Define variations of a saved baseline, review the resulting cases, and create them for execution from the project screen."}, {"url": "studies.html#study-and-baseline-controls", "page": "Parameter study screen", "title": "Study and baseline controls", "text": "Study and baseline controls Field or option What it does Study name Renames this study without requiring a rebuild. Baseline label Identifies the fixed set of inputs from which variations are made. Inspect baseline Opens those saved inputs read-only. Replace baseline Selects a different successful, unchanged, ready case as the fixed baseline. Add baseline as independent case When offered for an imported study, copies its saved baseline into a separate case. All combinations Generates every combination of the selected variation values. Explicit case rows Uses the particular combinations entered in the row table. Reusable definitions… Opens the saved bed and operating-program library."}, {"url": "studies.html#variation-fields-and-dialogs", "page": "Parameter study screen", "title": "Variation fields and dialogs", "text": "Variation fields and dialogs Field or option What it does Parameter / Values table Lists the varied parameters and their values; double-click a row to edit it. + Add variation Opens the parameter picker, including reusable program and bed replacements. Parameter entry Chooses the input to vary; its baseline value and units identify the field. Unavailable entries explain why. Edit variation Edits the selected parameter's values or definition choices. Values Enters an explicit list, separated by commas, spaces, or new lines. Range Generates a linearly spaced set from Start to End. Start / End First and last range values in the parameter's units. Number of values Number of values in the range. Expanded values Read-only preview of the resulting list, or a message explaining an unfinished rule. Reusable-definition checkboxes Selects the saved program or bed definitions used for a replacement variation."}, {"url": "studies.html#explicit-rows-and-preview", "page": "Parameter study screen", "title": "Explicit rows and preview", "text": "Explicit rows and preview Field or option What it does Explicit-row numeric cell Enters that case's value for the corresponding variation. Blank cells remain unfinished. Explicit-row definition selector Selects a reusable program or bed for that case. Candidate table Previews the cases produced by the rules; selecting a row shows its validation details. Inspect selected case Opens that candidate's inputs, bed, and program for inspection. Refresh preview Recomputes the candidate list from the current rules. Case count / replacement note Shows how many cases will be created and what an existing study rebuild will replace. Create N cases Creates the displayed cases without running them. Replace X cases with Y After confirmation, deletes the study's old generated cases and results and creates fresh cases."}, {"url": "studies.html#set-the-variations", "page": "Parameter study screen", "title": "Set the variations", "text": "Set the variations The baseline is a fixed copy of a successful case's inputs. Inspect baseline opens it read-only; Replace baseline selects another eligible successful case. Later edits to the source case do not change the saved baseline. Use + Add variation to choose a numerical parameter, reusable program, or reusable bed. Numerical variations accept explicit values or a spaced range. All combinations combines every variation value; Explicit case rows lets you enter particular pairings. Inspect the live case preview, including the bed and program. Complete unfinished variation rules before generation. Invalid scientific inputs can be generated as drafts, but must be resolved before execution."}, {"url": "studies.html#create-and-rebuild-cases", "page": "Parameter study screen", "title": "Create and rebuild cases", "text": "Create and rebuild cases Create N cases creates the inputs without running them. Return to the project screen to run the generated cases. Their scientific inputs are read-only; edit the study or duplicate a generated case as an independent case. Report layouts remain editable. Changing study rules marks its generated cases Needs update . Rebuilding replaces generated cases and their results after confirmation. Keep any run you need by exporting its results before rebuilding. Read more: Parameter studies · Rebuilding a study ."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#gui-guide", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "GUI guide", "text": "GUI guide This guide covers the MultiSolid desktop application: creating cases, running simulations, comparing results, and managing parameter studies. Desktop installation is covered in the installation guide ; source setup is in the repository README (online) . For scientific definitions, see the model equations and plugin guide . A project contains cases, studies, reusable definitions, and plugins. A case contains its inputs and one latest run . Editing scientific inputs keeps its existing results and marks them stale . Starting another run replaces that case's previous results, including when the new run later fails or is cancelled. Task Go to Define your own case Create and edit a case Run a case for the first time First run Choose a solver or run several cases Run simulations View plots and logs Inspect results Export one case to Excel Case Report Compare several cases in Excel Project Results Vary inputs systematically Parameter studies Transfer a project Share project inputs Recover unfinished edits Saving and recovery Resolve a problem Troubleshooting"}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#projects-and-navigation", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Projects and navigation", "text": "Projects and navigation The welcome screen offers Create new project , Open existing project , Import archived project , and recent projects. Opening a project resumes it in place. Creating or importing requires a new folder; an existing folder is never merged or overwritten. On Windows, the default parent is %USERPROFILE%\\Documents\\MultiSolid , independent of a Documents location redirected by OneDrive. On other systems it uses the OS Documents location, falling back to ~/Documents/MultiSolid . Browse… selects another parent, and the application remembers a custom creation location. Use a local, writable folder outside the application installation. Project archives can be transferred to synced storage when you want to share inputs. The Project menu provides creation, opening, saving, closing, folder access, archives, recent projects, and reusable definitions. Plugins opens the project's scientific definitions. The Help menu opens bundled HTML in your browser, including Getting started, the User guide, reference guides, and Troubleshooting. Help for this screen ( F1 ) opens a dedicated short page for the welcome screen, project screen, each case tab, Project Results, or parameter studies. Search help… opens the help home with search across the bundled pages. Help works offline, including the equations; links marked online require an internet connection. About MultiSolid shows application and component versions and lets you preview and copy diagnostic information. Licences and notices shows the application's notices. Each case row shows input readiness and the latest run status separately: Input status Meaning Ready Inputs and the required runtime pass the execution checks. Underdefined Some required values are unfinished. Invalid Inputs conflict or contain unsupported values. Runtime unavailable The selected backend or solver is missing a required component. Needs update The case belongs to a study that must be rebuilt. Not run , Queued , Running , Succeeded , Failed , Cancelled , and Interrupted describe the latest execution. A stale suffix means that run does not match the current scientific inputs or selected plugin contents. It can still be inspected when its files are available. Use the row's Run , Duplicate , Edit , and Delete icons. Double-click to edit; press F2 on a case name to rename it. Deleting asks before removing the case and its latest results. Study groups can be collapsed in the case list. Recent projects also appear under Project → Recent projects , with their locations and last-interaction times. Opening, editing, and starting execution update recency; background result writes do not. Unavailable folders are omitted. If you move or copy a project, open it once with Open existing project to add its new location. Opening a project elsewhere does not change the preferred parent for newly created projects."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#create-and-edit-a-case", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Create and edit a case", "text": "Create and edit a case Choose New Case and give it a name. It starts as an incomplete draft, so select the chemistry and supply the physical inputs before running it. Drafts save automatically, including unfinished values. New cases start in Feed mode with Repeat program enabled, 20 axial cells, WENO3 mass and heat schemes, and Compiled / Band execution with one thread and relative tolerance 1e-7 . Outlet composition and Outlet conditions plots and their required reports are selected. These defaults do not fill in the bed, species, feed, or a useful time horizon for you. The editor has five tabs: Tab What you define General Simulation duration, reporting interval, spatial discretization, solver, and recorded quantities/plots Chemistry Gas and solid species, reaction families, selected reactions, and role bindings Bed Geometry, heat transfer, material zones, voidages, and solid loadings Program Initial inlet conditions, outlet pressure, and timed hold/ramp steps Report An Excel workbook layout using recorded data For a new case, it is often easiest to complete Chemistry → Bed → Program , then review General . Red field borders and fills identify missing or invalid values; hover for an explanation. A red tab marker points to a tab with errors. Rules involving several values, such as composition totals, highlight the group. Details… explains errors that cannot be attached to a single field."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#general-numerics-and-recorded-outputs", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "General: numerics and recorded outputs", "text": "General: numerics and recorded outputs Set the simulation horizon, reporting interval, number of cells, and mass/heat schemes. The reporting interval controls saved samples; it is separate from the solver's internal time steps. For a non-repeating program, the horizon is derived from the timed steps and cannot be edited here. Choose Backend , Linear solver , Number of threads , and Relative tolerance . Advanced solver settings… exposes additional tolerances and integration controls. See Run simulations for availability and compiled execution. Requested reports selects the quantities to record. Requested plots selects plots and identifies the reports they require. Request the quantities you will need before running: changing an Excel layout later cannot create data that were never recorded. The Report tab arranges those data into worksheets; it does not control what the solver records."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#chemistry-species-and-reactions", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Chemistry: species and reactions", "text": "Chemistry: species and reactions Add gas and solid species from their respective pickers. Each definition shows its source. Choose + Add reaction families… and add the required models. Select the reactions to include. Use a family's + Species action to add missing required components. Inspect Bindings… when a role could refer to more than one selected component, such as two property variants of the same chemical. Set the new species' gas fractions in Program and solid loadings in Bed as needed. Added values default to 0 ; removal deletes the species' entries from compositions and zones. Selecting a family makes its reactions available; inspect the reaction checkboxes to choose what will run. Rates from overlapping selected models add. If you intend one model to replace another, remove the unwanted selection. The reaction graph updates in the background. Click a node to highlight its direct neighbours and connecting links; click again or click empty space to clear the highlight. Scroll or use + / − to zoom, drag to pan, and choose Fit to show the whole graph. The layout refits when the pane resizes. The graph uses Graphviz neato . Source installations can use it on PATH or set MULTISOLID_GRAPHVIZ to its executable. See the source installation instructions (online) if it is missing. The packaged desktop application includes Graphviz."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#bed-geometry-and-material-zones", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Bed: geometry and material zones", "text": "Bed: geometry and material zones Set the radius and length, ambient temperature, heat-transfer coefficient, gas-occupied bed fraction, and flow reversibility. Heat dispersion (W/m/K) sets effective axial heat spreading; 0 disables it. The implemented boundary conditions and transport equations explain these choices. Each material-zone row contains its start/end positions, interparticle and intraparticle voidages, particle diameter, and each selected solid's loading. Choose the Solid density basis (bed or solid volume) and Concentration units (mol/m³ or kg/m³) that match your data. Changing these selectors changes how the entered values are interpreted; it does not convert those numbers. The displayed wt% is calculated from the zone's initial loadings and selected molecular weights, not an additional input. The first zone starts at zero and the last ends at the bed length. + Add zone splits the final zone, defaults its solid loadings to 0 , and leaves its voidages and particle diameter blank. Complete the zones so they cover the bed without gaps or overlaps. Changing a multi-zone bed's length offers fixed or proportionally scaled internal boundaries. The preview below the settings displays the sampled bed profiles used by the engine. Use it to inspect your zones after changing boundaries or cell count."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#program-operating-conditions-over-time", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Program: operating conditions over time", "text": "Program: operating conditions over time Set the initial conditions first, then add timed steps. Mode How you specify the inlet Feed One feed state combines flow, temperature, and composition. A ramp can change selected parts together. Unticked values carry over from the preceding feed. Independent channels Flow, temperature, and composition have separate step lists. Outlet pressure has its own channel in both modes. Choose Hold to retain the current state for a duration, or Ramp to reach a target over that duration. Targets display their units. Switching modes preserves each mode's inlet steps; the first visit to another mode copies the initial feed values and starts with no inlet steps. Outlet pressure is shared. In a composition dialog, new species values default to 0 . Set non-negative mole fractions for the gases present so the total is one. Normalize scales a positive total to one while preserving ratios. It becomes available once all entries are valid and their total is positive. Repeat program repeats each channel's step list, with an editable overall horizon in General . Each cycle carries forward the previous cycle's final values; add a ramp back to the starting state if the cycle should return there. For a non-repeating program, the longest channel sets the horizon, and every non-empty channel must have the same duration. A non-repeating program with no timed steps needs at least a hold or ramp to define its horizon. Hand-authored CLI inputs can instead specify an explicit horizon with all channels constant. Flow basis switches between mol/s and GHSV (h⁻¹). This conversion uses the bed geometry and reference conditions of 273.15 K and 100,000 Pa . Set a positive radius and length before converting existing numeric flows. The preview shows the engine's smoothed schedules, repetition, and GHSV conversion. Feed transitions interpolate molar flow, species molar flows, and flow × temperature; see Operating programs . Use a channel's arrow to collapse it, or Focus to give it more room; Show all restores the channels. Preview toolbars support zoom, pan, and image saving."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#first-run", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "First run", "text": "First run Once you have completed the case inputs in the tabs above: Choose ← Back to project and check that the case shows Ready . If it does not, open the case and inspect the highlighted inputs or Details… . Choose the case's Run icon. Hover over the icons for their labels. After Succeeded , open the case, go to General , and choose Show beside an available requested plot. Use Report to prepare an Excel export. Checkpoint: the case list shows a successful run and the case can display its retained results. Use Open latest run log if execution fails. To compare a changed case with this result, duplicate the original and edit the copy. The original keeps its result; the duplicate starts with copied inputs and report layout but no results. If you prefer to start with existing case files, choose Import Case and select their run.yaml . Keep its referenced YAML files alongside it in their original layout. The application copies the inputs into your project and leaves the originals unchanged. Review the imported case's tabs before running it. For users who have the source repository, the default case (online) is a complete example. It uses Standard / SuperLU , which importing preserves."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#run-simulations", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Run simulations", "text": "Run simulations Return to the project case list and use a case's Run icon. For a batch, check the cases to include and choose Run all included cases . All included cases are validated before any execution begins; uncheck unfinished drafts. A failure in one running case does not stop the remaining cases. Maximum workers controls simultaneous cases and is saved with the project. Each case keeps its own thread setting. The two controls are independent: raising the worker limit does not edit a case's numerical settings. Elapsed time updates while a case is active and freezes when it finishes. Cancel execution stops active workers and their children and cancels the queue."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#choose-a-backend-and-solver", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Choose a backend and solver", "text": "Choose a backend and solver Backend Offered linear solvers What to expect Standard SuperLU, KLU, LAPACK (dense), AztecOO with ILUT / Ifpack ILU / ML, SUNDIALS GMRES with Ifpack ILU Uses the installed DAETools solver adapters. Compiled SuperLU, KLU, Band Builds a native model and reuses compatible compiled models. Availability depends on the installed runtime and compiler. The dropdown explains unavailable choices. Switching to an incompatible backend or solver asks before updating affected settings; Cancel keeps the original inputs. Imported advanced settings are preserved. An unsupported selection blocks execution instead of silently substituting another solver. Both backends need DAETools; Compiled uses it for model construction and initial conditions. Source installations need the matching adapters and native runtime described in the installation guide (online) . The Windows distributions include their compiler, Graphviz, and solver libraries. KLU factorization is serial on both backends."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#compiled-progress-and-caching", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Compiled progress and caching", "text": "Compiled progress and caching A first compiled run may spend several minutes generating and compiling its model. Progress distinguishes cache checks, waiting for compilation, generation, and compilation from actual integration. Later compatible cases reuse project/.packed_bed_cache/ , including cases in studies. Different operating schedules can share a compiled model while keeping their own program data. The cache survives reruns and is excluded from project archives. It can be deleted while the project is idle and will be rebuilt when needed. The project must be writable. For source builds, see the compiled runtime guide (online) ."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#what-a-rerun-replaces", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "What a rerun replaces", "text": "What a rerun replaces Once a case starts, the new attempt replaces its previous input snapshot, logs, status, and outputs. A later failure or cancellation does not restore the old run. A failed preflight, or cancellation while a case is still queued, leaves its previous results intact. Duplicate a case before editing or running a comparison if you want the original result to remain available. Duplicates copy inputs and the Report definition, then start with Not run . Prepared runs use fixed input and plugin copies, so later edits cannot change the inputs associated with a retained result."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#inspect-results", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Inspect results", "text": "Inspect results Open the case and use General → Requested plots → Show for a separate plot window. Available retained NetCDF data can be plotted even when stale. Plot toolbars provide pan, zoom, and image saving. Open case folder opens the case's files; its run/ folder contains the latest snapshot, solver log, and outputs. Open latest run log opens worker.log . Outputs include NetCDF data, the engine provenance manifest, and any generated plots. Choose Report for one case's Excel workbook, or Results… in the project case list to compare successful runs. A stale result still describes its retained run's inputs, not your current draft. Reports include those retained inputs and provenance. Run snapshots and output manifests retain the MultiSolid engine/UI versions, source commit, and packaged build identifier. Excel information sheets include this retained identity even when exported with a newer application. To identify the application currently open, use Help → About MultiSolid → Copy diagnostic information , which includes the source commit and build identifier."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#export-a-case-to-excel", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Export a case to Excel", "text": "Export a case to Excel Report opens on the first user worksheet, starting with a blank sheet for a new configuration. Name it and choose what its rows represent (time by default, or any available coordinate axis). Add columns… selects a quantity and values for its other axes. Three species at three positions create nine columns; remove, reorder, or rename individual columns after adding them. Every column heading includes the value unit. Hover over a preview heading to read its full definition. Coordinates and available quantities always follow the current case inputs. Row selections support All, First, Last, and Selected values. All columns regenerate when the grid changes; surviving columns keep their labels and order, and manually removed positions stay excluded. First/Last follows the endpoint. Manually selected coordinates that no longer exist are removed with a notice; unfinished inputs preserve selections until coordinates can be calculated again. Missing quantities remain visible for repair. When results exist, the preview shows recorded values and coordinates from the retained run, limited to 20 rows and 12 columns; export contains the full selection. Case information also shows the retained run's inputs and provenance. Before a run, the preview shows expected coordinates and headers and explains why value cells are empty. Editing controls continue to follow current inputs, including when retained results are stale. Older saved reports stored every generated coordinate as an exact selection. Re-add those columns with All to enable automatic grid updates; the old format does not record whether they were originally selected individually or with All. Export workbook… writes native Excel Tables, with the header row and first column frozen. The fixed first sheet, Case information , contains the retained run's settings, provenance, and an exact dictionary of the exported columns. Stale or partial results can be exported when the selected data and their input snapshot are available. No data are interpolated or silently dropped. Export can be cancelled and only replaces an existing workbook after success. Reports autosave with the project independently of scientific inputs. Editing a report does not make results stale, and reports remain editable on generated study cases. Duplicating a case copies its report definition without results. Save as template… writes a portable JSON layout. Apply template… previews its sheets, sizes, and any compatibility issues before replacing the current configuration. Each applied template is an independent editable copy. Templates contain no results, case identity, or run provenance."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#compare-cases-in-project-results", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Compare cases in Project Results", "text": "Compare cases in Project Results Click Results… beside Run all included cases . A dialog first asks which cases to make available in Results. Only cases whose latest run succeeded and whose result files are present can be selected. Select cases individually or check a study group; filtering also matches factor selections. Select all and Clear all affect every eligible case, including cases hidden by the filter. These checkboxes are independent of Run all inclusion. Cancel keeps the project case list open. Select cases… inside Results reopens the same dialog. The worksheet controls work like the case Report tab, with Case as another axis. Choose what rows represent, then Add columns… : Time rows with five cases and three outlet species give 15 value columns. Position rows compare profiles at explicitly selected recorded times. Case rows compare individual recorded values. Time defaults to Last ; First/Last resolves separately for each case. This does not calculate an average. Shared rows must have the same recorded coordinates. For different grids, use Split by case to replace the current sheet with a separate, editable worksheet for each case, or select row coordinates recorded in all referenced cases. The export never interpolates or silently matches nearby samples. Cell positions and face positions remain separate axes. All expands to explicit columns when added, using each case's own grid. Saved columns keep their case identities and coordinate selections. Missing cases or coordinates remain visible for repair after a rerun or study rebuild. First/Last follows the retained run's endpoints; add columns again to include new cases or coordinates. Refresh results reloads the current retained data. Preview shows up to 20 rows and 12 columns; Export workbook… exports the full selection as native Excel Tables. The fixed Results information sheet includes run identities, study selections, retained inputs, status, staleness, available balance diagnostics and exact per-case coordinates for every value column. Successful but stale results can be exported if their selected data and snapshot are readable. Missing quantities or samples block the affected worksheet with a reason. Save as template… saves the worksheet layout as JSON, without run data or case identities. Apply template… previews the sheets, sizes, compatibility and case mapping before replacing the layout. Select the same number of cases as the template; its case positions map to the selected cases in their displayed order. Applied templates are independent copies, and edits do not change the template file. For a failed run, open the individual case's Report tab to export any saved partial data. A normal solver failure can retain samples recorded before the failure. These show the state at reporting times, not rejected solver iterations or necessarily the exact failure instant. A hard crash, forced stop, or failure before recording may leave no exportable data; consult Open latest run log too. Use Excel for your own averages, integrals and plots. Project Results layouts autosave and support draft recovery independently of case Report definitions and scientific inputs. Export can be cancelled; an existing workbook is replaced only after success. Existing individual-case plot windows remain available."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#parameter-studies", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Parameter studies", "text": "Parameter studies Run the intended baseline case successfully first. New Parameter Study accepts only a case whose latest run succeeded, whose current inputs are ready, and whose successful snapshot still matches those inputs. The study copies those inputs as a fixed baseline, records the successful attempt, and does not copy results. Inspect baseline is read-only; Replace baseline selects another successful case. Subsequent changes to the source case do not change the saved baseline. Selected plugins still use the project's current contents for future runs; see Project plugins . The baseline also retains a copy of its report configuration. Every generation or rebuild copies that layout independently into each fresh case, with All axes resolved against that case's inputs. Older studies without a saved report layout capture their source report on their next rebuild, if the source is still available. Study rows have edit and delete icons. Deleting a study asks for confirmation, then removes its generated cases and results while keeping independent cases and reusable definitions. The study workspace contains variations beside a live case preview: + Add variation selects a named parameter with units, or a reusable program or bed configuration. Numerical variations accept value lists or linearly spaced ranges. Program fields include initial values, selected step durations, and ramp targets. Step references survive earlier-step deletion and never move silently to another step. All combinations produces the Cartesian product. Explicit case rows specifies individual pairings, with definition dropdowns and numeric cells. The row table supports spreadsheet copy/paste, duplicate, move, remove, and undo/redo. Blank cells remain unfinished. Length sweeps scale all solid-zone boundaries proportionally. Program and bed replacements cannot overlap numerical variations inside the replaced inputs. Timing, species, compositions, geometry, and solver choices use engine validation. Inspect a preview row for the same program and bed plots available in the case editor. Invalid scientific inputs can become draft cases; ambiguous or unfinished variation rules must be repaired before generation. Previews are cancellable. Create N cases writes cases without running them. Generated inputs are read-only; use Edit study or Duplicate as independent case . For example, three programs × three bed configurations produce nine cases. With independent Benchmark and Exploratory cases, the project has 11 cases. Benchmark must have succeeded before it is selected as the study baseline. Definitions and the copied baseline do not add simulations to the count."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#rebuilding-a-study", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Rebuilding a study", "text": "Rebuilding a study Autosave stores the study rule without changing its generated cases. Changing the baseline, variations, or a referenced definition marks the study Needs rebuild and all its generated cases Needs update . Those cases cannot run until rebuilt; existing results remain inspectable. Reverting the changes clears this requirement. Renaming a study or definition does not require a rebuild. Replace X cases with Y deletes every previous generated case in that study, including its inputs, snapshots, logs, and results, then creates fresh cases with new identities, generated names, and default inclusion. Even unchanged combinations are replaced. The preview states the case and result counts before this action. There are no archived cases or restoration. Rebuilds preserve independent cases, other studies, the fixed baseline, and the reusable-definition library."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#reusable-programs-and-beds", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Reusable programs and beds", "text": "Reusable programs and beds Use Project → Reusable definitions… , or create/edit definitions while choosing a study variation. Programs contain the operating program, its mode and repetition setting. Bed configurations contain length, radius, ambient temperature, heat-transfer coefficient (U), axial heat dispersion, gas voidage mode, flow reversibility, and solids. The existing input controls edit these definitions with explicit Save / Cancel . Inherited inputs provide preview context. Unused definitions remain in the library; deleting a referenced definition is blocked until its study references are removed. Older bed definitions continue inheriting any settings they do not yet contain. Editing and saving them captures these settings from the displayed inputs. A bed variation cannot be combined with a numerical sweep of a setting that bed owns. Create desktop studies with New Parameter Study using a successful baseline case. Run all included cases executes included independent and generated cases together, using the project's Maximum workers setting and each case's thread count."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#cli-batches-and-older-studies", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "CLI batches and older studies", "text": "CLI batches and older studies Engine batch.yaml files are used through the CLI ( python -m packed_bed batch --help ), and cannot be imported as desktop studies. The desktop still supports importing an individual case's run.yaml and importing complete project archives. Studies imported by earlier versions remain readable and retain their saved rules. For those existing advanced studies, only settings overridden by the original batch preset apply; other settings still come from the baseline. A pending imported study can still use Add baseline as independent case and Replace baseline to establish a successful baseline. No saved cases or results are removed by retiring the batch-import action."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#share-project-inputs", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Share project inputs", "text": "Share project inputs Choose Project → Export project… to save a .msproject archive. This includes every case (including unfinished drafts), reusable definitions, parameter studies, Report/Results layouts and registered plugins. Results, run snapshots, logs and local settings are excluded. Save the archive outside the project folder; a cancelled or failed export leaves an existing archive unchanged. Choose Project → Import project archive… , available even before opening a project. Select the archive, then choose a project name and parent location using the normal creation form. The destination must be a new folder. A successful import opens the project and adds it to Recent projects. Imported cases start at Not run ; their saved report layouts can be used again after running the cases. Established study baselines and studies awaiting rebuild retain their state. Archives carry the current plugin catalogue, including unused/disabled plugins. Importing and browsing never execute supplied Python code or grant local approval. Use Plugins → Allow code… / Enable when needed. Missing/incompatible packages, damaged archives and unsafe file paths block import with an explanation. Transfers run in a cancellable dialog while the project is idle."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#project-plugins", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Project plugins", "text": "Project plugins Open Plugins beside Project . Register a .msplugin archive or a plugin folder's manifest.yaml , enable it, and select its species or families in Chemistry . Registration alone does not change case selections. Plugins in an imported project archive are already registered. Enabled data-only plugins work immediately. For a Python plugin marked Needs local approval , choose Allow code… and wait for Checks passed. Disabled plugins remain disabled until enabled. Importing and browsing do not execute plugin Python. Approval permits code to run with your user permissions during checks and simulations; workers are not a security sandbox. Approval stays on this computer. Code/resource changes require approval again; manifest parameter edits do not. Cases and studies use the current contents of their selected project plugins for future runs, including studies with a fixed baseline. Retained run snapshots keep their original plugin copies. The plugin guide explains variants, replacement, updates, and sharing; the example walkthrough teaches plugin creation."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#saving-and-recovery", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Saving and recovery", "text": "Saving and recovery Case inputs, Report definitions, Project Results layouts, and study rules save after a short pause, including unfinished values. Project → Save Project , switching projects, and closing flush pending edits, including the active table cell. If saving fails, the draft stays in the editor and navigation is blocked; read the reason in the status bar and resolve the write problem before leaving. Edits create a recovery checkpoint in .drafts/ before the autosave delay. After an interruption, opening the project offers: Choice Effect Recover drafts Restore the unfinished edits and reassess readiness and staleness. Discard drafts Keep the saved inputs and discard the recovery edits. Cancel Leave the recovery decision pending without opening the project. Recovery preserves retained run snapshots and results. It does not resume a solver. Unfinished retained runs are marked Interrupted after reopening. Project and execution locks prevent concurrent access. An input archive excludes run data. To retain a full project with its latest results, close it and copy the entire project folder. Each case still has only one latest run; the copied folder does not provide a history of earlier attempts."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#troubleshooting", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting Problem What to check Run is unavailable Check input readiness, highlighted tabs, and Details… . Complete the draft or resolve its runtime requirement. Run all refuses to start Every included case must pass validation. Uncheck unfinished cases or rebuild cases marked Needs update . First compiled run seems slow Check whether progress says generating or compiling. The first build can take several minutes; later compatible runs use the cache. Solver is unavailable Read the dropdown's explanation. For a source installation, check DAETools adapters, compiler, and native runtime setup. Chemistry graph is missing Check Graphviz neato or MULTISOLID_GRAPHVIZ , then use Retry in the graph pane. A composition will not normalize Enter every species value, keep them non-negative, and ensure the total is positive. Program horizon cannot be edited Non-repeating programs derive it from timed steps. Match all non-empty channel durations or enable repetition and set the horizon. A new species makes the case incomplete Gas fractions and solid-zone loadings default to 0 . Check that gas fractions sum to one and inspect reaction bindings. A plugin has no effect Select its definitions in Chemistry or use the plugin replacement action. See the plugin guide . Results are stale Inputs or selected plugin contents have changed. Inspect/export the retained run, or run the case again for current inputs. A plot or workbook lacks a quantity Check whether that quantity was recorded in the retained run. Request it in General and rerun if needed. Cases cannot share a Results worksheet Select exactly shared recorded row coordinates or use Split by case . Export does not interpolate. A failed run is missing from Project Results Use that case's Report tab for any saved partial samples and inspect its log. Project Results selects successful runs. A study baseline cannot be selected Run a ready case successfully and keep its inputs unchanged before selecting it. A study case cannot be edited Edit the study or duplicate the generated case as an independent case. Report layouts remain editable. Saving or opening is blocked Read the status message, check write access, and close another process using the project. Preserve the open draft until saving succeeds. A moved project is missing from Recent projects Open its new location with Open existing project ."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#appendix-project-files-and-developer-reference", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Appendix: project files and developer reference", "text": "Appendix: project files and developer reference project/ project.json # name, cases, studies, max_workers, registered plugins plugins/<plugin-id>/current/ # current registered plugin files .drafts/ # recovery checkpoints for unfinished edits .packed_bed_cache/ # reusable compiled models; excluded from archives cases/<case-id>/ inputs/ # run/program/solids/chemistry.yaml for this case run/ # one latest execution, replaced on rerun inputs/ # fixed inputs associated with the latest result snapshot.json # input hashes, fingerprint, case/attempt identity status.json worker.log output/ # NetCDF, engine manifest, and plots studies/<study-id>/ study.json # rule, fixed-baseline provenance, stable step targets baseline/ # four copied scientific documents batch.yaml # compatibility: rule saved by an older batch import definitions/<definition-id>/ # metadata plus program.yaml or solids.yaml execution.json # latest Run case / Run all queue status execution.log # worker startup diagnostics Prepared snapshots briefly live in .pending-<attempt> beside a case's run/ . Once validated, a snapshot replaces that case's run folder; .previous-run is a short-lived backup during the directory swap. Recovery completes an interrupted swap. Attempt identities distinguish worker events; they are not run history. Input/metadata files use atomic replacement. Study and definition writes use a staged directory transaction. Rebuilding commits project metadata only after staging the complete replacement case set. Before that commit, recovery restores old cases; after it, recovery deletes temporary backups. Recovery runs before loading case inputs. Project archives transfer saved definitions and exclude recovery checkpoints and run data. File publication and temporary-directory cleanup share the engine's file_io.py (online) policy. Status files, results, manifests, cache records, plugin files and exports use unique temporary files before atomic replacement. Brief Windows reader locks are retried for up to 0.75 seconds; permanent access errors still surface. Run/study folder swaps use the same retry policy while retaining their existing rollback order. Export cancellation is checked again before each replacement attempt. Temporary-file and scratch-directory cleanup preserve the original failure if cleanup also fails. The application reads the current project format (3) and run-snapshot format (1). Older project formats are rejected rather than migrated. Project and solver locks prevent concurrent access. Reopening after an interrupted session marks unfinished retained runs as Interrupted. File Responsibility window.py (online) Welcome screen, project case list, navigation, and actions case_list.py (online) Collapsible study groups, case status, inclusion, and icon actions editor.py (online) Shared input controls, read-only inspection, case autosave, and retained plots studies.py (online) Parameter catalogue, pure expansion, validation, and generation signatures study_store.py (online) Baseline eligibility, persistence/imports, and transactional complete rebuilds study_editor.py (online) Study workspace, variation dialogs, and cancellable previews study_tables.py (online) Preview and explicit-row models, spreadsheet paste, and definition dropdowns definition_editor.py (online) Reusable-definition library and shared inspection dialogs inputs.py (online) Explicit draft initialization, input validation, timing, and zone scaling general.py (online) Numerical/solver settings and report/plot selections chemistry.py (online) Species lists and reaction families reaction_graph.py (online) Asynchronous Graphviz SVG preview, node highlighting, zoom and pan bed.py (online) Geometry, material zones, and reactor boundary rules program_editor.py (online) Program modes, hold/ramp tables, feed targets, timing, and flow conversion editor_widgets.py (online) Shared selection lists, tables, and preview canvases project.py (online) Project/case storage, imports, readiness, and run preparation execution.py (online) Start, monitor, and stop the project's worker with Qt worker.py (online) Verify/activate snapshots, collect logs, publish status, call the shared engine loop results.py (online) Project Results, using the shared Report worksheet controls project_results.py (online) Case-axis selections, native-grid validation and project Excel export batch.py (online) Shared case scheduling, concurrency, thread limits, and worker cleanup preview.py (online) Scientific preview arrays without Qt or plotting imports Study selectors and the project library share DefinitionList and its editing actions. Both the workspace and storage use preview_study() ; its lazy mode lets Qt consume one captured preview incrementally. Shared document access, scientific fingerprint inputs, timing, and scaling live in inputs.py . ProjectCase.resolve() uses the engine's metadata-only inspect_case() . Workers load the snapshots and call run_case() with its optional status callback. The UI and batch CLI both use run_cases_in_processes() for concurrent scheduling and worker cleanup. The CLI retains its in-process path for one worker with no timeout. Each project stores one current copy of each plugin; Save replaces that copy. Code/resource approval belongs to the local computer and survives parameter edits. Workers reconstruct authoritative definitions from plugin copies in their input snapshots. See the plugin guide for workflows and the API."}, {"url": "gui-guide.html#interface-assets", "page": "GUI guide", "title": "Interface assets", "text": "Interface assets The interface follows system light/dark appearance and uses a shared numeric font. Maratype supplies the wordmark, project title, and M/S ribbon; its font notice (online) stays beside the asset. The launch splash appears before the workspace loads; solver workers run without it. Documentation index"}, {"url": "equations.html#model-equations", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Model equations", "text": "Model equations This guide describes the reactor model implemented by MultiSolid-CL. The equations below use the same concentration bases, face fluxes, boundary conditions, and source-term conventions as PackedBedModel (online) . Standard and Compiled execution start from this model; their numerical treatment is described in Time integration . The core balances apply to every selected chemistry. Individual kinetic laws and property datasets are supplied by built-in definitions or plugins. Their validity ranges and provenance remain part of those definitions; see Reaction sources ."}, {"url": "equations.html#contents", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Contents", "text": "Contents Scope and assumptions Notation and grid Volume fractions and concentration bases Species balances and reaction sources Thermodynamic and transport properties Gas transport and pressure drop Energy balance Boundary conditions Operating programs Initial conditions Spatial reconstruction Time integration Conservation diagnostics and reported quantities Configuration and implementation map"}, {"url": "equations.html#scope-and-assumptions", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Scope and assumptions", "text": "Scope and assumptions The reactor is a fixed cylindrical bed represented in one axial dimension. Cross-sectional variations are unresolved. The implemented assumptions are: Gas is an ideal mixture. Each cell has one pressure, gas composition, and temperature. Gas and solids share that temperature: local thermal equilibrium is assumed, with no separate gas-to-solid heat-transfer equation. Solids are stationary. Their inventories change through reactions, with no axial solid transport. Gas moves by convection and axial dispersion. Bed radius, bed length, interparticle and intraparticle voidages, and particle diameters are prescribed and remain fixed during a run. Reaction-induced changes in solid inventory do not change voidage or geometry. The gas storage volume can include particle pores, but there is no separate pore-gas state or resolved intraparticle diffusion problem. Particle effects such as an effectiveness factor must be represented by the selected kinetics. The pressure gradient follows an algebraic Ergun closure. There is no momentum accumulation equation, gravity term, or independent prescribed interior flow. Species enthalpies depend on temperature and include their reference enthalpies. Stored energy is internal energy, with ideal-gas pressure-volume correction and negligible solid pressure-volume contribution. Kinetic and gravitational potential energy are omitted. Heat exchange with the environment is represented by a constant overall wall coefficient and ambient temperature. An optional constant axial heat-dispersion coefficient acts on the shared temperature. There is no separate wall energy state, resolved radial conduction, or radiation term. These assumptions define the implemented model. In particular, selecting a reaction family does not add a separate particle, wall, or gas-temperature model."}, {"url": "equations.html#notation-and-grid", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Notation and grid", "text": "Notation and grid Use SI units unless a particular kinetic correlation explicitly converts them. The gas constant in the implementation is R = 8.31446 J m o l − 1 K − 1 . Symbol Meaning Unit or basis L , R b , A = π R b 2 Bed length, radius, cross-sectional area m, m, m² j , i , a , k Cell, gas species, solid species, reaction indices — e b , e p Interparticle voidage; pore fraction within particles Dimensionless ε g , ε s Modelled gas volume and solid skeleton volume per bed volume Dimensionless c i , s a Gas and solid concentrations stored by the model mol/m³ bed C i = c i / ε g Gas-phase concentration mol/m³ gas c t = ∑ i c i , C t = c t / ε g Total gas concentrations on the two bases mol/m³ bed; mol/m³ gas y i = c i / c t Gas mole fraction Dimensionless r k , S i g , S a s Reaction-extent rate and species source terms mol/(m³ bed·s) u f Superficial gas velocity at a face m/s, based on total area A N i , f Gas species molar flux at a face mol/(m² bed·s) h i g , h a s Species molar enthalpies J/mol J i , f , q f Species enthalpy flux; axial dispersive heat flux W/m² bed E j Cell internal energy per bed volume J/m³ bed P , T , ρ g , μ g Gas pressure, shared temperature, gas density, mixture viscosity Pa, K, kg/m³ gas, Pa·s D f , d p , f Axial mass-dispersion coefficient; particle diameter m²/s, m U , T ∞ , λ a x Wall heat-transfer coefficient, ambient temperature, axial heat dispersion W/(m²·K), K, W/(m·K) Positive flux is from the inlet at x = 0 to the outlet at x = L . Mathematical cell indices here run from 1 to n ; Python array indices run from 0 to n − 1 . The configured grid is uniform, with at least three cells: Δ x = L n , x j = ( j − 1 2 ) Δ x , x j − 1 / 2 = ( j − 1 ) Δ x . Concentrations, temperature, pressure, and gas properties live at cell centres. Velocities, particle diameters, dispersion coefficients, and transport fluxes live at faces. Define the conservative cell divergence of a face quantity as D j ( F ) = F j + 1 / 2 − F j − 1 / 2 Δ x j , Δ x j = x j + 1 / 2 − x j − 1 / 2 . The balances retain this cell-width form, but the supplied grid builder and higher-order reconstruction formulas use a uniform grid. The distance used in an interior face gradient is x j + 1 − x j ; the inlet and outlet pressure-drop segments are half a cell wide."}, {"url": "equations.html#volume-fractions-and-concentration-bases", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Volume fractions and concentration bases", "text": "Volume fractions and concentration bases The model.gas_voidage_mode setting defines gas storage: ε g = { e b + ( 1 − e b ) e p , bed_and_particle (default) , e b , bed_only . ε s = ( 1 − e b ) ( 1 − e p ) . bed_only excludes pore gas from storage while retaining the same solid skeleton fraction. Consequently ε g + ε s need not equal one in that mode: the excluded pore volume is not reassigned to solids. The chosen gas fraction is used in initialization, the equation of state, transport concentrations, and energy storage. Ergun resistance always uses e b . Solids are specified in contiguous zones covering the bed. Zone voidages and initial solid values are sampled at cell centres; a narrow zone containing no cell centre does not contribute a cell inventory. Choose a resolution that represents the intended zones. Particle diameters are sampled at faces. A face exactly on a zone boundary uses the arithmetic mean of the two adjacent zone diameters. These rules are implemented in solid_profiles.py (online) . Let v a be an entered solid value and M a its molecular weight in kg/mol. The conversion to the model state is s a , 0 = b { v a , mol_per_m3 , v a / M a , kg_per_m3 , b = { 1 , basis: bed , ε s , basis: solid . Thus a value per solid volume is multiplied by the solid skeleton fraction; mass inputs are divided by molecular weight first. Outputs and reaction source terms continue to use mol/m³ bed regardless of the input basis. No evolving solid-density or volume-filling closure is imposed."}, {"url": "equations.html#species-balances-and-reaction-sources", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Species balances and reaction sources", "text": "Species balances and reaction sources"}, {"url": "equations.html#gas-and-solid-balances", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Gas and solid balances", "text": "Gas and solid balances For each gas species and cell, d c i , j d t + D j ( N i ) = S i , j g . For each stationary solid species, d s a , j d t = S a , j s . There is no extra voidage multiplier on these accumulation or source terms: c i , s a , and both sources already use total bed volume. The total concentrations and mole fractions are algebraic closures: c t , j = ∑ i c i , j , s t , j = ∑ a s a , j , y i , j c t , j = c i , j . Gas mole fractions sum to one when the total gas concentration is nonzero and the closures are satisfied. Total solid concentration is not held constant."}, {"url": "equations.html#stoichiometric-source-terms", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Stoichiometric source terms", "text": "Stoichiometric source terms Each selected reaction has one extent rate r k , j . Its signed stoichiometric coefficients ν i k g and ν a k s are negative for reactants and positive for products: S i , j g = ∑ k ν i k g r k , j , S a , j s = ∑ k ν a k s r k , j . The reaction network builds these matrices from the selected definitions in reactions.py (online) . Sources from all selected reactions add; selecting two mechanisms for the same conversion does not make one replace the other. With no reactions, all source terms are zero. For example, a reaction written as F e 2 O 3 + 3 H 2 → 2 F e + 3 H 2 O gives S H 2 g = − 3 r , S H 2 O g = 3 r , S F e 2 O 3 s = − r , and S F e s = 2 r . The scalar r is an extent rate; the stoichiometric coefficients are applied once by the source matrices. They do not determine kinetic reaction orders. A kinetic hook receives the cell state and returns a symbolic rate in mol/(m³ bed·s). A correlation defined per gas volume, catalyst mass, or another basis must perform its own conversion to that output basis. The core model does not automatically multiply hook results by voidage or catalyst loading. Partial pressures follow p i = y i P ; correlations using bar or other units must convert from the model's pascals. Catalyst roles can affect a rate without having a stoichiometric source term. Clamping, gas-availability gates, conversion limits, and regularization constants belong to individual kinetic implementations. There is no universal reaction-rate clip or limiter that guarantees nonnegative concentrations. Transient solver trial states can leave a correlation's physical domain."}, {"url": "equations.html#reaction-sources", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Reaction sources", "text": "Reaction sources Family Implementation Copper on silica, San Pio form copper_sio2.py (online) Copper on alumina, including spinel chemistry copper_al2o3.py (online) Nickel redox, Medrano form nickel_medrano.py (online) Nickel-catalysed reforming, Xu–Froment form reforming_xu_froment.py (online) Nickel-catalysed reforming, Numaguchi–Kikuchi form reforming_numaguchi.py (online) Iron redox, He form iron_he.py (online) The kinetics source notes identify scientific references and implementation-specific choices. The additional waste iron plugin documents its own rate laws and property fits. Plugin and built-in variants use the same core balances; changing their rates or properties can change both composition and temperature."}, {"url": "equations.html#thermodynamic-and-transport-properties", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Thermodynamic and transport properties", "text": "Thermodynamic and transport properties"}, {"url": "equations.html#equation-of-state-and-density", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Equation of state and density", "text": "Equation of state and density The ideal-gas closure is P j ε g , j = c t , j R T j , C t , j = P j R T j . With gas molecular weights M i in kg/mol, M m i x , j = ∑ i y i , j M i , ρ g , j = P j M m i x , j R T j = ∑ i C i , j M i . This is density per gas volume, as needed by Ergun. Multiplying it by ε g gives gas mass per bed volume."}, {"url": "equations.html#species-enthalpy-and-heat-capacity", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Species enthalpy and heat capacity", "text": "Species enthalpy and heat capacity Every selected gas or solid species supplies h ( T ) . Consistent heat capacity is C p ( T ) = d h / d T . Reference enthalpies must use a consistent chemical reference because reaction energy is obtained from these species enthalpies. For PolynomialHeatCapacity , with coefficients a m and θ = T − T r e f , C p ( T ) = ∑ m = 0 p a m θ m , h ( T ) = h r e f + ∑ m = 0 p a m m + 1 θ m + 1 . The coefficients are in ascending powers of T − T r e f , not powers of absolute T . The parameter h_form_ref supplies h r e f in J/mol. For ShomateHeatCapacity , let T * = 1000 K , τ = T / T * , and τ r e f = T r e f / T * : C p ( T ) = a 0 + a 1 τ + a 2 τ 2 + a 3 τ 3 + a 4 τ − 2 , h ( T ) = h r e f + T * [ & a 0 ( τ − τ r e f ) + a 1 2 ( τ 2 − τ r e f 2 ) + a 2 3 ( τ 3 − τ r e f 3 ) & + a 3 4 ( τ 4 − τ r e f 4 ) − a 4 ( τ − 1 − τ r e f − 1 ) ] . This implementation anchors the integrated heat capacity at the explicitly supplied reference enthalpy; it does not take a separate pair of Shomate enthalpy-offset coefficients. Custom correlations supply numerical and symbolic enthalpy/heat-capacity functions. Scientific validity and agreement between those functions must be established for the chosen temperature range."}, {"url": "equations.html#viscosity", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Viscosity", "text": "Viscosity The built-in pure-species QuadraticViscosity correlation is μ i ( T ) = a 0 , i + a 1 , i ( T − T r e f , i ) + a 2 , i ( T − T r e f , i ) 2 . Mixture viscosity uses Wilke's rule: μ g = ∑ i y i μ i ∑ ℓ y ℓ ϕ i ℓ , ϕ i ℓ = [ 1 + μ i / μ ℓ ( M ℓ / M i ) 1 / 4 ] 2 8 ( 1 + M i / M ℓ ) . The diagonal factor is exactly one. The implementation does not divide by mole fractions, so absent species with zero mole fraction are supported when the property values and remaining mixture are valid. Initialization and the evolving model use the same function. See properties.py (online) and the IDAES description of Wilke's rule (online) ."}, {"url": "equations.html#gas-transport-and-pressure-drop", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Gas transport and pressure drop", "text": "Gas transport and pressure drop"}, {"url": "equations.html#interior-species-fluxes", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Interior species fluxes", "text": "Interior species fluxes At an interior face f = j + 1 / 2 , D f = 1 2 | u f | d p , f , C ― t , f = 1 2 ( c t , j ε g , j + c t , j + 1 ε g , j + 1 ) , N i , f = A f ( u f , C i ) − D f C ― t , f y i , j + 1 − y i , j x j + 1 − x j . Here A f is the scheme-dependent convective transport operator defined in Spatial reconstruction . The dispersed gradient is mole fraction, multiplied by the arithmetic face mean of total gas-phase concentration. It is not the gradient of concentration per bed volume. The mass-dispersion coefficient is common to the gas species and contains no separate molecular-diffusion term. It vanishes at zero velocity. The dispersive species fluxes sum to zero when both neighbouring compositions sum to one. The boundary species fluxes are prescribed separately below; the interior formula is not evaluated across an external ghost cell."}, {"url": "equations.html#ergun-closure", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Ergun closure", "text": "Ergun closure Define the drag function E f ( u ) = 150 μ f ( 1 − e b , f ) 2 e b , f 3 d p , f 2 u + 1.75 ρ f ( 1 − e b , f ) e b , f 3 d p , f | u | u . For each interior face, P j − P j + 1 x j + 1 − x j = E f ( u f ) . Viscosity, density, and interparticle voidage at an interior face are arithmetic means of the adjacent cell values. Particle diameter follows the face-sampling rule above. Boundary drag uses the adjacent cell's viscosity, density, and interparticle voidage. The signed | u | u term permits either velocity sign. Velocity and pressure are solved together with species and energy accumulation; total molar flow need not remain equal to the inlet flow along a reacting, transient bed. The interior_flow_mode setting changes transport reconstruction, not this pressure-drop equation."}, {"url": "equations.html#energy-balance", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Energy balance", "text": "Energy balance"}, {"url": "equations.html#stored-internal-energy", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Stored internal energy", "text": "Stored internal energy The differential energy state is E j = ∑ i c i , j h i g ( T j ) + ∑ a s a , j h a s ( T j ) − ε g , j P j . The subtraction is the ideal-gas pressure-volume contribution per bed volume. Using the equation of state, the same definition is E j = ∑ i c i , j [ h i g ( T j ) − R T j ] + ∑ a s a , j h a s ( T j ) . Temperature is recovered through this algebraic relation and the species enthalpy closures. It is shared by gas and solids. Holding the gas and solid concentrations fixed, the derivative of this stored energy with respect to temperature is ∑ i c i ( C p , i g − R ) + ∑ a s a C p , a s ."}, {"url": "equations.html#enthalpy-transport-and-axial-heat-dispersion", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Enthalpy transport and axial heat dispersion", "text": "Enthalpy transport and axial heat dispersion At an interior face, the enthalpy flux of gas species i is J i , f = A f ( N i , f , h i g ) , H f = ∑ i J i , f . This uses the complete species molar flux, including its dispersive part. The heat_scheme reconstructs each species enthalpy. In reversible mode, its upwind direction follows the sign of that species flux, which need not be the same as the sign of superficial velocity. The additional axial heat flux is q f = − λ a x T j + 1 − T j x j + 1 − x j at interior faces , q 1 / 2 = q n + 1 / 2 = 0 . The constant model.axial_heat_dispersion_w_per_m_k is nonnegative and defaults to zero. The coefficient and heat flux use the total bed cross section; no additional gas or solid volume fraction multiplies this expression. This term can conduct heat at zero flow and is independent of the velocity-dependent mass-dispersion coefficient."}, {"url": "equations.html#wall-exchange-and-cell-balance", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Wall exchange and cell balance", "text": "Wall exchange and cell balance The volumetric heat loss to the surroundings is Q w a l l , j = 2 U R b ( T j − T ∞ ) . The factor 2 / R b is the cylindrical sidewall-area-to-bed-volume ratio. Positive Q w a l l removes energy; it becomes a heat input when the surroundings are hotter. The model uses one constant U and one T ∞ for the whole bed. The energy equation is d E j d t + D j ( H ) + D j ( q ) + Q w a l l , j = 0 . When λ a x = 0 , the heat-dispersion divergence is omitted. Internal face heat transfers enter neighbouring cells with opposite signs. Since dispersive heat flux is zero at both boundaries, this term redistributes stored energy without providing an external heat source."}, {"url": "equations.html#how-reaction-heat-enters", "page": "Model equations", "title": "How reaction heat enters", "text": "How reaction heat enters There is no additional − Δ H r r term in the solved energy balance. Reactions change the species inventories, and the stored energy includes those species' reference and sensible enthalpies. Adding a second reaction-heat source to this formulation would count the same contribution twice. To see the connection, define C p , b e d , j = ∑ i c i , j C p , i g ( T j ) + ∑ a s a , j C p , a s ( T j ) , Δ H k ( T ) = ∑ i ν i k g h i g ( T ) + ∑ a ν a k s h a s ( T ) . Differentiating the energy definition at fixed voidage and substituting the species balances gives the equivalent identity C p , b e d , j d T j d t − ε g , j d P j d t = & − D j ( H ) − D j ( q ) − Q w a l l , j & + ∑ i h i g ( T j ) D j ( N i ) − ∑ k Δ H k ( T j ) r k , j . This identity explains the implicit reaction heat and pressure correction; it is not an extra equation assembled by the solver. The implementation integrates E j directly. In particular, storing total enthalpy instead of E j would omit the pressure contribution during transients."}, {"url": "equations.html#boundary-conditions", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Boundary conditions", "text": "Boundary conditions The operating program prescribes total inlet molar flow F i n ( t ) > 0 , inlet composition y i , i n ( t ) , inlet temperature T i n ( t ) , and outlet pressure P o u t ( t ) . Inlet pressure is solved, not supplied independently."}, {"url": "equations.html#inlet", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Inlet", "text": "Inlet N i , 1 / 2 = F i n A y i , i n , J i , 1 / 2 = N i , 1 / 2 h i g ( T i n ) , q 1 / 2 = 0 . The inlet velocity and pressure obey F i n A = u 1 / 2 P i n R T i n , P i n − P 1 x 1 − 0 = E 1 / 2 ( u 1 / 2 ) . This is a prescribed total species-flux and enthalpy-flux boundary. The first cell's composition and temperature are not fixed to the inlet values during integration. No additional species dispersion flux is added to the prescribed N i , 1 / 2 ."}, {"url": "equations.html#outlet", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Outlet", "text": "Outlet N i , n + 1 / 2 = u n + 1 / 2 c i , n ε g , n , J i , n + 1 / 2 = N i , n + 1 / 2 h i g ( T n ) , q n + 1 / 2 = 0 , P n − P o u t L − x n = E n + 1 / 2 ( u n + 1 / 2 ) . Outlet species and enthalpy fluxes use the final cell directly, with no added axial species-dispersion flux. There is no independently imposed outlet composition or temperature. Solids have no boundary flux at either end."}, {"url": "equations.html#flow-reversal-limits", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Flow reversal limits", "text": "Flow reversal limits simulation.interior_flow_mode: reversible switches interior reconstruction according to flux direction. Both inlet and outlet equations above remain unchanged. There is no external outlet-reservoir composition or temperature to supply a physical backflow boundary. Reversible interior transport should therefore not be interpreted as a model of interchangeable inlet/outlet ports. forward_only always uses the forward-oriented face reconstruction. It does not clamp negative velocity or remove the signed Ergun closure; cases with interior reversal require the reversible reconstruction."}, {"url": "equations.html#operating-programs", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Operating programs", "text": "Operating programs Programs consist of an initial value and timed hold or ramp steps. A hold keeps the preceding value; a ramp has an absolute target. Channels without steps remain constant. Flow, temperature, and pressure inputs are positive; each specified composition includes every selected gas species and sums to one."}, {"url": "equations.html#separate-channels-and-feed-streams", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Separate channels and feed streams", "text": "Separate channels and feed streams In separate_channels mode, flow, temperature, composition, and outlet pressure are independently programmed. In feed_stream mode, the inlet program instead interpolates the molar quantities F , G i = F y i , B = F T . After interpolation and smoothing, y i , i n ( t ) = G i ( t ) F ( t ) , T i n ( t ) = B ( t ) F ( t ) . All inlet quantities use the same positive flow denominator. This preserves species molar-flow interpolation when the total flow changes. The temperature is weighted by molar flow, not by an enthalpy balance between two feed streams. A ramp target may omit flow, temperature, or composition; omitted values carry forward from the preceding feed. Outlet pressure remains a separate channel."}, {"url": "equations.html#ramp-smoothing", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Ramp smoothing", "text": "Ramp smoothing The default smoothing half-width is w = 1 s . Define the smoothed positive part of elapsed time z by g w ( z ) = { 0 , z ≤ − w , ( z + w ) 2 / ( 4 w ) , − w < z < w , z , z ≥ w . A ramp from a to b between t s and t e contributes ( b − a ) g w ( t − t s ) − g w ( t − t e ) t e − t s to the carried value. Each corner is smoothed only within w of its endpoint; outside those regions, the result agrees with the unsmoothed piecewise-linear program. If short steps have overlapping smoothing regions, the same summed formula applies. Values and first derivatives are continuous at the joins. For feed streams the smoothing is applied to F , G i , and B before taking ratios. A ramp starting at time zero can therefore alter the actual inlet state at t = 0 from its declared initial values. Initialization, previews, and both solver backends use these smoothed conditions. The smoothing width is an engine parameter, not a field in the current YAML schema. With repeat_program: true , each channel's next cycle starts from its previous cycle's final value; it does not reset to the initial value. The first cycle and the carried-forward continuation are retained, and extending the simulation horizon does not change earlier program values. Repeating channels use their own step-list durations. For non-repeating inputs, each channel with steps must span the configured horizon; channels without steps remain constant."}, {"url": "equations.html#ghsv-conversion", "page": "Model equations", "title": "GHSV conversion", "text": "GHSV conversion When the flow basis is ghsv_per_h , the volumetric flow uses the empty bed volume V b = A L and the reference conditions T N = 273.15 K , P N = 100000 P a : F i n = G H S V 3600 V b P N R T N . No voidage factor multiplies V b . This conversion is shared by both program modes. Program construction is implemented in programs.py (online) ."}, {"url": "equations.html#initial-conditions", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Initial conditions", "text": "Initial conditions initialization.py (online) constructs the starting state before DAETools solves for consistency: Build the uniform axial grid, sample the solid zones, convert solid inputs to mol/m³ bed, and calculate the fixed volume fractions and face diameters. Evaluate the smoothed programs at zero to obtain F 0 , y i , 0 , T 0 , and P o u t , 0 . The initializer requires positive inlet flow. Evaluate the mixture properties at T 0 and y i , 0 . Construct a positive-flow Ergun pressure-profile seed using the inlet molar flux F 0 / A , face properties, and half-cell boundary distances. Bracket and bisect the inlet pressure until the constructed outlet pressure matches P o u t , 0 . Set every cell's initial temperature and gas composition to T 0 and y i , 0 , while allowing pressure and gas concentration to vary axially: c i , j ( 0 ) = y i , 0 ε g , j P j ( 0 ) R T 0 , s a , j ( 0 ) = s a , 0 , j , E j ( 0 ) = ∑ i c i , j ( 0 ) h i g ( T 0 ) + ∑ a s a , j ( 0 ) h a s ( T 0 ) − ε g , j P j ( 0 ) . Gas and solid concentrations and internal energy are supplied as differential initial conditions. Pressure, temperature, fluxes, rates, and other algebraic quantities receive initial guesses. DAETools' SolveInitial() then obtains consistent algebraic values and differential derivatives before either backend integrates. The pressure construction is an initial-state calculation, not a claim that the reactive bed is initially at steady state or chemical equilibrium. There is no independently specified axial initial gas-composition or temperature profile in the current case format. Cumulative inlet/outlet mass and energy transfers and cumulative wall heat loss start at zero. Initial bed inventories are calculated from the initialized concentrations and internal energy."}, {"url": "equations.html#spatial-reconstruction", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Spatial reconstruction", "text": "Spatial reconstruction simulation.mass_scheme and simulation.heat_scheme independently select how interior face states are reconstructed. Species convection reconstructs C i = c i / ε g ; enthalpy transport reconstructs h i g ( T ) . For a generic cell quantity v , let v f L and v f R be reconstructions from the left and right. For signed transport rate a f , A f ( a f , v ) = { a f v f L , forward_only , a f + v f L + a f − v f R , reversible , a f ± = a f ± | a f | 2 . Use a f = u f for species convection and a f = N i , f for species enthalpy transport. Right reconstructions apply the same formulas to the reversed cell sequence. Central reconstruction is identical from either direction."}, {"url": "equations.html#upwind-central-and-limited-linear-schemes", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Upwind, central, and limited linear schemes", "text": "Upwind, central, and limited linear schemes At the face between cells j and j + 1 , the left reconstructions are: Scheme v f L upwind1 v j central ( v j + v j + 1 ) / 2 linear_upwind2 3 v j / 2 − v j − 1 / 2 muscl_minmod v j + minmod ( v j − v j − 1 , v j + 1 − v j ) / 2 The limiter returns the argument of smaller magnitude if both differences have the same sign and zero otherwise. At the first interior face, schemes needing a second upstream cell fall back to upwind1 ."}, {"url": "equations.html#weno3", "page": "Model equations", "title": "WENO3", "text": "WENO3 For v j − 1 , v j , v j + 1 , the candidate states and smoothness indicators are p 0 = 3 2 v j − 1 2 v j − 1 , p 1 = 1 2 ( v j + v j + 1 ) , β 0 = ( v j − v j − 1 ) 2 , β 1 = ( v j + 1 − v j ) 2 . With d 0 = 1 / 3 , d 1 = 2 / 3 , α m = d m ( β m + ϵ 2 ) 2 , ω m = α m ∑ ℓ α ℓ , v f L = ∑ m ω m p m ."}, {"url": "equations.html#weno5", "page": "Model equations", "title": "WENO5", "text": "WENO5 For v j − 2 , v j − 1 , v j , v j + 1 , v j + 2 , p 0 & = 1 3 v j − 2 − 7 6 v j − 1 + 11 6 v j , p 1 & = − 1 6 v j − 1 + 5 6 v j + 1 3 v j + 1 , p 2 & = 1 3 v j + 5 6 v j + 1 − 1 6 v j + 2 , β 0 & = 13 12 ( v j − 2 − 2 v j − 1 + v j ) 2 + 1 4 ( v j − 2 − 4 v j − 1 + 3 v j ) 2 , β 1 & = 13 12 ( v j − 1 − 2 v j + v j + 1 ) 2 + 1 4 ( v j − 1 − v j + 1 ) 2 , β 2 & = 13 12 ( v j − 2 v j + 1 + v j + 2 ) 2 + 1 4 ( 3 v j − 4 v j + 1 + v j + 2 ) 2 . Use the same nonlinear-weight formula with ( d 0 , d 1 , d 2 ) = ( 0.1 , 0.6 , 0.3 ) . Where its full stencil is unavailable, weno5 falls back to weno3 ; where the WENO3 upstream stencil is unavailable, it falls back to upwind1 . The same boundary fallback rules apply to the reversed right reconstruction. The regularization scale is ϵ = 10 − 8 m o l / m 3 for gas-phase concentration and 10 − 8 J / m o l for enthalpy. It enters as ϵ 2 in the smoothness denominator. It is a reconstruction parameter, not a concentration floor or a solver absolute tolerance. These formulas, including their boundary fallbacks, are in axial_schemes.py (online) . Higher-order reconstruction and central transport do not guarantee positive concentrations. Mass and heat dispersion continue to use centred differences regardless of the selected convective reconstruction."}, {"url": "equations.html#time-integration", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Time integration", "text": "Time integration The spatial balances and algebraic closures form a differential-algebraic system F ( t , z , z ˙ ) = 0 . Gas and solid inventories and internal energy are differential states; optional cumulative balance reports add differential accounting states. Fluxes, composition closures, pressure, properties, and reaction rates are algebraic quantities in the unreduced model. Standard ( solver.backend: daetools ): DAETools assembles and integrates the model using IDAS and the selected linear-solver adapter. Compiled ( solver.backend: compiled ): DAETools first solves the same initial conditions. The backend exports the equations, substitutes explicit algebraic definitions, eliminates fixed states where applicable, and generates native residuals, analytic Jacobians, and report reconstruction. SUNDIALS IDA integrates the reduced system with SuperLU, KLU, or Band. The intended physical balances and boundary conditions are shared. Algebraic elimination changes the solver state vector and error control, so identical settings do not imply bitwise-identical trajectories. See simulation.py (online) , the compiled backend (online) , and its graph reduction (online) . IDA uses variable-step, variable-order backward differentiation, with orders from one to five. Its error weights have the form W ℓ = 1 r t o l | z ℓ | + a t o l ℓ . The resulting weighted error norm controls local integration error, not a bound on the error in a final scientific result. The numerical method is described in the SUNDIALS IDA mathematical reference (online) . solver.concentration_absolute_tolerance sets the gas and solid concentration tolerances in mol/m³ bed; the total-concentration closures use the same types. Other variable types retain their own tolerances. Absolute tolerance is particularly important for trace species, where the relative term is small. maximum_order , nonlinear convergence controls, and suppress_algebraic_errors affect solver behaviour, not the physical source terms. Suppressing algebraic error estimates does not remove the algebraic equations. Compiled residual scaling likewise changes numerical conditioning while retaining the residual zeros. Both backends allow up to 30 consecutive nonlinear convergence failures while reducing a time step, instead of IDA's default of ten. This lets sharply varying rates near reactant depletion recover at a smaller step without relaxing the case's error tolerances or accepting an unconverged solution. A persistent failure still stops the run. reporting_interval_s sets saved output times, not a fixed integration step. The reporting schedule includes zero and the final horizon, adding a shorter last reporting interval if needed. Selecting plots does not change the balances. Selecting mass/heat balance reports adds their accounting equations and can therefore affect the numerical path even though the physical reactor equations are unchanged."}, {"url": "equations.html#conservation-diagnostics-and-reported-quantities", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Conservation diagnostics and reported quantities", "text": "Conservation diagnostics and reported quantities"}, {"url": "equations.html#mass-accounting", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Mass accounting", "text": "Mass accounting The optional mass_balance report stores M b e d = A ∑ j Δ x j ( ∑ i M i c i , j + ∑ a M a s a , j ) , d M i n d t = A ∑ i M i N i , 1 / 2 , d M o u t d t = A ∑ i M i N i , n + 1 / 2 . Its signed reported error is δ M = M b e d ( t ) − M b e d ( 0 ) − M i n ( t ) + M o u t ( t ) . Summing cell balances cancels the interior fluxes exactly at the equation level. Zero physical mass error also requires each selected reaction to satisfy ∑ i M i ν i k g + ∑ a M a ν a k s = 0 with the actual selected molecular weights. A nonzero diagnostic can therefore reflect stoichiometry/property inconsistency as well as numerical error. The core source assembly does not repair an unbalanced reaction."}, {"url": "equations.html#energy-accounting", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Energy accounting", "text": "Energy accounting The optional heat_balance report stores E b e d = A ∑ j Δ x j E j , d Q i n d t = A H 1 / 2 , d Q o u t d t = A H n + 1 / 2 , d Q l o s s d t = A ∑ j Δ x j Q w a l l , j . Its signed reported error is δ E = E b e d ( t ) − E b e d ( 0 ) − Q i n ( t ) + Q o u t ( t ) + Q l o s s ( t ) . Despite the output name heat_bed_total , the stored inventory is internal energy . heat_in_total and heat_out_total accumulate boundary enthalpy transfer. Axial dispersive heat contributes no boundary transfer, and reaction heat is already included through the species enthalpies. The cumulative quantities are integrated as part of the DAE, not estimated afterwards by trapezoidal integration of saved samples. All transfers retain their signs. These diagnostics are reported in kg and J. The summary identifies the largest absolute error among saved reporting times, not necessarily its largest value between those times. Good conservation alone does not establish accurate kinetics, adequate spatial resolution, or valid property data."}, {"url": "equations.html#interpreting-derived-outputs", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Interpreting derived outputs", "text": "Interpreting derived outputs reports.py (online) derives the following quantities from the retained model data: Outlet temperature is the last cell's temperature, T n . Pressure drop is P i n − P o u t , including both boundary half-cell segments. Outlet species flow is A N i , n + 1 / 2 and total outlet flow is its sum. Outlet composition is N i , n + 1 / 2 / ∑ ℓ N ℓ , n + 1 / 2 . When the magnitude of the total outlet flux is at most 10 − 12 m o l / ( m 2 s ) , the reported composition falls back to the final-cell mole fractions. Solid mole fraction is s a / ∑ b s b when total solid concentration is positive; an empty solid inventory reports zero fractions. This is a derived quantity, not an additional dynamic state or a solid volume fraction. For numerical comparisons, vary the axial grid and solver tolerances, compare both conservation errors and quantities of interest, and retain the selected chemistry and property definitions with the results."}, {"url": "equations.html#configuration-and-implementation-map", "page": "Model equations", "title": "Configuration and implementation map", "text": "Configuration and implementation map Input setting Model meaning model.bed_length_m , bed_radius_m , axial_cells L , R b , and the uniform axial grid model.gas_voidage_mode Gas storage fraction ε g model.ambient_temperature_k T ∞ ; omitted-field default 873.15 K model.heat_transfer_coefficient_w_per_m2_k U ; omitted-field default 100 W/(m²·K); set zero for no wall exchange model.axial_heat_dispersion_w_per_m_k λ a x ; default zero solids.initial_profile Concentration unit/basis and zones supplying initial solids, e b , e p , and d p simulation.mass_scheme , heat_scheme Interior concentration and enthalpy reconstruction simulation.interior_flow_mode Forward-oriented or sign-aware interior transport simulation.program_mode , repeat_program Boundary-program construction and repetition simulation.time_horizon_s , reporting_interval_s Integration horizon and saved reporting times solver.* Backend, linear solver, tolerances, and numerical controls outputs.requested_reports Saved quantities and optional integral balance equations Input paths in this table refer to the corresponding run.yaml or solids.yaml sections. Defaults are schema fallbacks; authored examples and new desktop cases can specify different values. The current definitions are in config/models.py (online) , with cross-file validation in config/load.py (online) . Implementation Responsibility model.py (online) Balances, source sums, state closures, fluxes, and boundary equations solid_profiles.py (online) Grid, volume fractions, zone sampling, and concentration conversion properties.py (online) Enthalpy, heat capacity, pure viscosity, and Wilke mixing reactions.py (online) Reaction definitions and stoichiometric source matrices kinetics/ (online) Built-in symbolic reaction rates programs.py (online) Smoothing, repetition, feed mixing, and GHSV conversion initialization.py (online) Initial pressure seed, inventories, and algebraic guesses axial_schemes.py (online) Reconstruction stencils, nonlinear weights, and signed flux splitting simulation.py (online) DAETools initialization and execution compiled/runtime.py (online) Native IDA integration and solver controls reports.py (online) Output coordinates, derived quantities, and balance errors Documentation index"}, {"url": "plugins.html#plugin-guide", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Plugin guide", "text": "Plugin guide Plugins add species, property equations, and reaction models to MultiSolid. Start with the smallest change you need: changing coefficients usually needs no Python; writing a new equation does. What you want to do Start here Change an existing species or reaction parameter Create a variant in the application Define a new species using the existing property equations Create a species plugin Write a new property equation or reaction rate Plugin authoring walkthrough Understand the files and Python API Authoring reference Use a plugin someone sent you Install and use a plugin Send your plugin to someone else Share and update plugins Fix a problem Troubleshooting The example walkthrough builds a complete plugin one file at a time. It starts with a species in YAML, then adds a Python property equation and a reaction. The examples use illustrative coefficients and kinetics; they are teaching material, not validated scientific models."}, {"url": "plugins.html#choose-what-to-define", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Choose what to define", "text": "Choose what to define A plugin is a folder containing manifest.yaml and, when needed, Python files. The manifest gives the plugin a name and describes what it provides. An exported .msplugin file packages that folder for sharing. Part Meaning Example Species A chemical identity, phase, molecular weight, and property definitions NH3 gas Correlation An equation that calculates a property from temperature Molar enthalpy and heat capacity Reaction family One or more reactions, with their stoichiometry and rate equations Ammonia synthesis Parameter A value that users can change without editing an equation A rate coefficient k You do not need to write Python for every species. Polynomial or Shomate heat capacity and quadratic gas viscosity are already available. Use the application forms or a manifest to supply their coefficients. Use Python when those equations cannot express your property model, or when you need a new rate law."}, {"url": "plugins.html#create-a-variant-in-the-application", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Create a variant in the application", "text": "Create a variant in the application This is the quickest way to try different coefficients for an existing model. You need only the desktop application and an open project. For example, to give nitrogen a different heat capacity: Open Plugins , beside Project in the menu bar. Expand the built-in definitions and select Nitrogen . Use the filter to find it if necessary. Choose Create variant… and enter a recognizable New plugin name , such as My nitrogen properties . Choose Polynomial Cp under Heat capacity / enthalpy . Set Tref (K) to 298.15 , H at Tref (J/mol) to 0 , and Cp coefficients a0, a1, … to 30, 0.004, 0.000001 . These are illustrative values. Keep the chemical key N2 and phase gas to make this a nitrogen replacement. Save. The application checks the definition and creates an enabled plugin. Select the new plugin's Nitrogen definition and choose Use this as replacement… . Choose the existing nitrogen definition and review the affected cases before applying the change. The last step connects your definition to cases that already use nitrogen. If the project has no such cases, add the new definition in a case's Chemistry tab instead. Creating or enabling a plugin alone does not change any case. The same workflow works for reaction parameters: select a built-in reaction or family, choose Create variant… , edit its exposed values, and save. Select the family entry when replacing an existing family in cases. A reaction's editor exposes only its declared parameters; changing stoichiometry or adding a new rate equation requires Python authoring. Double-click a definition in your own plugin to edit it. Double-click a built-in definition to create a variant. Selecting a family shows all its parameters; selecting a reaction shows its parameter group when one is available. Shared parameters are marked. Reset shown values restores implementation defaults. Save checks and commits the edit. Invalid values remain in the editor with an explanation. Cancel , Escape, or closing the editor discards unsaved edits. Saving unchanged values does not create another copy."}, {"url": "plugins.html#create-a-species-plugin", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Create a species plugin", "text": "Create a species plugin For a new species, choose Plugins → Create plugin… , name it, then select the plugin and choose Add species… . The application generates the internal IDs. Fill in these fields: Field What to enter Name A readable label, such as Ammonia Chemical key The chemical identity used to match reaction roles, such as NH3 Phase gas or solid Molecular weight kg/mol , so 17.03 g/mol becomes 0.01703 kg/mol Minimum / maximum T The temperature interval in K for which your definition is intended Heat capacity / enthalpy An equation type, its coefficients, and reference enthalpy Gas viscosity Quadratic coefficients in the displayed SI units; required for gas species Scientific source / Notes Data source, fit assumptions, and limitations Save, then add another species if needed. A plugin may contain several species. Custom enthalpy correlations already declared in that plugin also appear in the species form. Their parameters are editable there. Custom viscosity code and its configuration are edited in the source package. Prefer text files? Lesson 1 provides a complete manifest.yaml to copy. There is no Python file, build step, or metadata-generation step for that example."}, {"url": "plugins.html#property-units-and-coefficient-conventions", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Property units and coefficient conventions", "text": "Property units and coefficient conventions Temperatures are in K, molecular weights in kg/mol, molar enthalpies in J/mol, heat capacities in J/(mol·K), and viscosities in Pa·s. The declared temperature_range documents the intended range. It does not clamp temperatures or stop a simulation outside that range. Check your case's results against the validity ranges of its selected definitions. Built-in equation Manifest model Parameters Polynomial heat capacity builtin:polynomial coefficients , t_ref , h_form_ref Shomate heat capacity builtin:shomate a0 through a4 , t_ref , h_form_ref Quadratic gas viscosity builtin:quadratic_viscosity a0 , a1 , a2 , t_ref For polynomial Cp, with ΔT = T − t_ref : Cp(T) = a0 + a1 ΔT + a2 ΔT² + … h(T) = h_form_ref + a0 ΔT + (a1/2) ΔT² + (a2/3) ΔT³ + … Coefficients are in ascending order. Coefficient ai has units J/(mol·K^(i+1)); three coefficients give quadratic Cp. These are powers of T − t_ref , so coefficients fitted to powers of T need conversion first. For Shomate Cp, τ = T / (1000 K) and Cp = a0 + a1 τ + a2 τ² + a3 τ³ + a4/τ² . Coefficients use J/(mol·K). MultiSolid integrates this expression and anchors enthalpy at h_form_ref at t_ref ; do not paste a different source's full enthalpy coefficient set into these fields. For quadratic viscosity, μ = a0 + a1 ΔT + a2 ΔT² . The coefficients have units Pa·s, Pa·s/K, and Pa·s/K² respectively. Its reference temperature may differ from the enthalpy reference temperature. Use consistent enthalpy references across reacting species. See the model equations for how properties and reaction source terms enter the balances."}, {"url": "plugins.html#install-and-use-a-plugin", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Install and use a plugin", "text": "Install and use a plugin Open the destination project and choose Plugins → Register plugin… . Select the supplied .msplugin archive, or select manifest.yaml inside the plugin folder. Keep its Python files and resources beside it. A plugin using only built-in equations is checked and enabled immediately. For a Python plugin, select it and choose Enable , then allow its code if you trust its source. Wait for Checks passed. In your case's Chemistry tab, add the plugin's species or reaction family from the appropriate picker. Select the reactions you intend to run. Check the family Bindings… and supply the corresponding gas compositions in Program and solid loadings in Bed . + Species can add missing components; it does not supply their compositions or loadings. Registration copies the folder into the project. Later edits to the original folder do not update the project copy; register it again after editing. Normal desktop use does not require a separate Python installation or a pip command for the plugin."}, {"url": "plugins.html#replace-a-definition-or-add-a-separate-component", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Replace a definition or add a separate component", "text": "Replace a definition or add a separate component Use Use this as replacement… when you want an existing case component to use new properties. Select the new species or family in Plugins, then choose the existing definition it should replace. Review the listed ordinary cases and reusable definitions. Component IDs, family instance IDs, compositions, and role bindings are preserved. A replacement species must have the same chemical key and phase. A replacement family must have the same ordered gas/solid roles and reaction IDs. The action does not change study selections or saved runs. Adding both original water and a water variant in Chemistry creates two distinct components, each with its own ID, composition, and balance equation. Use Bindings… to choose which component serves each reaction role. Likewise, selecting two reaction variants makes their selected rates add; remove a family you do not want to run. The editor shows an applicability caution for overlapping reactions."}, {"url": "plugins.html#python-approval-and-checks", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Python approval and checks", "text": "Python approval and checks The browser reads the manifest without importing plugin Python. Check and Enable execute it in a separate process; the application asks for local code approval first. An enabled plugin copied from another computer may show Needs local approval : choose Allow code… and wait for the check to finish. Approved code runs with your user permissions. The worker is not a security sandbox, and passing checks does not establish safety or scientific accuracy. Approval covers code and included resources. Changing manifest parameter values does not request approval again; changing code or resources does. Check failures and details appear in the browser or editor. Cancel check stops a pending check. Checks validate package structure, parameter declarations, factory return types, reaction metadata, and basic property construction. They do not compare every numerical and symbolic expression or validate a reactor model."}, {"url": "plugins.html#share-and-update-plugins", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Share and update plugins", "text": "Share and update plugins To share the version you edited in the application, select its plugin entry and choose Export… . Send the resulting .msplugin file. The recipient registers it in their project. You can also share the entire project folder; its plugins travel with it, while Python approval stays on each computer. For updates, keep the same plugin id and register the new manifest or archive. The application asks before replacing changed contents. To keep two alternatives, use Create variant… , or give your source copy a new id before registering it. Changing just the display name does not create an independent plugin. Each project stores one current copy at plugins/<plugin-id>/current/ . There is no revision selector and no required version increment. Edit your authoring folder and re-register, or use the application forms and export their result. Avoid treating the project copy as a live link to your source folder. Cases and studies use the current contents of their selected plugins. Changing a selected plugin makes previous results stale; a plugin edit alone does not require a new study baseline or a study rebuild. An unrelated plugin edit does not affect those results. Removing or disabling a plugin still used by inputs is blocked, with its users listed. Prepared runs keep copies of their selected plugins beside their input snapshot. Later edits cannot change that snapshot. Generated CLI batch cases also carry their selected plugin files. Include a source citation, applicable temperature range, assumptions, and any redistribution terms with a plugin you distribute."}, {"url": "plugins.html#authoring-reference", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Authoring reference", "text": "Authoring reference Follow the worked tutorial for complete files and commands. This section explains the contract when you adapt them to your own model. Python authoring commands use the source environment (online) , with the repository root as the working directory."}, {"url": "plugins.html#files-and-names", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Files and names", "text": "Files and names my_plugin/ ├── manifest.yaml # Identity, species, and implementation references ├── enthalpy.py # Optional custom property equation ├── kinetics.py # Optional reaction family and rates ├── data/ # Optional local resources └── README.md # Scientific basis and usage notes manifest.yaml must be at the root of the folder or archive. Reference a Python factory as module:function : kinetics:create calls create(parameters) in kinetics.py . A nested module can use models.kinetics:create . Manifest field Meaning id Stable plugin identity, such as my_ammonia name Human-readable plugin name description , source Purpose and provenance species Map from local species IDs to property definitions correlations Map from local correlation IDs to custom implementations mechanisms Map from local family IDs to kinetics implementations derived_from Optional attribution to an original definition format_version and api_version currently default to 1 ; version defaults to the string \"1\" . The current workflow does not use version fields to select historical plugin contents. Plugin and local definition IDs start with a letter and use letters, digits, underscores, dots, or hyphens. Choose portable names, avoiding Windows device names and trailing dots. builtin is reserved as a plugin ID. Keep these three names distinct: Name Example Purpose Definition reference my_ammonia:NH3 Plugin ID plus local species ID Chemical key / reaction role NH3 Matches a species to a chemical role Case component ID NH3 or NH3_variant Identifies the component in case compositions and balances Two definitions can have the same chemical key but different references. Case bindings choose which selected component supplies that role. Quote YAML strings such as 'NO' , including map keys, because the YAML reader interprets unquoted NO as a boolean."}, {"url": "plugins.html#write-declarations-once-generate-browsing-metadata", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Write declarations once, generate browsing metadata", "text": "Write declarations once, generate browsing metadata The manifest lets the application show a Python plugin's parameters and reactions without importing its code. Authors write those declarations in Python, then generate their manifest copies. You do not need to maintain both by hand. Write PARAMETERS and create(parameters) in your Python module. Put its implementation reference in the manifest. For a correlation, also set kind to enthalpy or viscosity . Run generate_metadata(folder) . It fills parameter declarations and, for a reaction family, the gas roles, solid roles, and reactions. Check the package, then register its completed manifest or exported archive. The tutorial build script performs metadata generation, checking, and packaging with one command. Generation imports your Python modules, executes reaction factories, and rewrites manifest.yaml ; use it on your own authoring files. It is not needed for a species plugin that uses only built-in property equations. Changing Python parameter declarations or reaction metadata requires regeneration; changing a species' parameter values does not."}, {"url": "plugins.html#parameters-and-factories", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Parameters and factories", "text": "Parameters and factories Import the supported types from packed_bed.plugins . Its exports include ParameterSpec , parameter_group , BaseCorrelation , PolynomialHeatCapacity , ShomateHeatCapacity , QuadraticViscosity , ReactionDefinition , ReactionFamily , and KineticsContext . Declare editable scalar parameters in a module-level dictionary: from packed_bed.plugins import ParameterSpec PARAMETERS = { \"k\": ParameterSpec( 0.001, \"m^3 bed/(mol*s)\", \"Rate coefficient using concentrations per bed volume\", minimum=0, ), } Every parameter needs a default and unit or explicit dimensionless basis. Bounds are optional and inclusive. The factory receives validated, immutable values. Use factory arguments and context.parameters , rather than modifying global constants. Only declared parameters appear in the application editor. The three places parameters appear have different jobs: Location What it stores Python PARAMETERS and generated manifest declarations Defaults, units, descriptions, and bounds species.<id>.enthalpy.parameters or .viscosity.parameters Values for that species' correlation mechanisms.<id>.values Overrides for that plugin reaction family's declared defaults Two species can share correlation code and have different parameter values. The CO2/H2O example (online) demonstrates this. Dotted declarations created with parameter_group resolve into nested parameter mappings; for example, oxidation.k becomes parameters[\"oxidation\"][\"k\"] ."}, {"url": "plugins.html#property-equations", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Property equations", "text": "Property equations A property factory returns a BaseCorrelation subclass. Implement these methods: Method Enthalpy correlation returns Viscosity correlation returns value(T) Numerical h in J/mol Numerical μ in Pa·s dae_expression(T) Symbolic h with units Symbolic μ with units cp_value(T) Numerical Cp in J/(mol·K) Not used cp_dae_expression(T) Symbolic Cp with units Not used Numerical methods accept scalar temperatures and NumPy arrays, preserving array shape. Symbolic methods accept DAETools expressions; use DAETools functions and unit-bearing constants there. Keep solver imports inside symbolic methods so metadata generation and ordinary numerical checks can run without the solver. Require dh/dT = Cp and h(t_ref) = h_ref . Check finite values over your intended range, positive Cp and viscosity where applicable, and agreement between the numerical and symbolic implementations. Registration is not a full scientific self-test. Lesson 2 includes a numerical consistency check."}, {"url": "plugins.html#reaction-equations", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Reaction equations", "text": "Reaction equations A kinetics factory returns a ReactionFamily with a name, reaction definitions, required gas and solid roles, and a map from each reaction ID to its rate function. The map needs exactly one callable for every reaction. A ReactionDefinition provides id , name , phase ( gas_gas , gas_solid , or solid_solid ), stoichiometry , required_species , and source_reference . Negative stoichiometric coefficients consume a species; positive ones produce it. Declare all species read by a rate in required_species , including catalysts or other non-stoichiometric dependencies. List catalysts in catalyst_species without including them in stoichiometry. The rate receives a KineticsContext . Use: context.model and context.idx_cell for the local model state; context.gas_index(role) and context.solid_index(role) for bound indices; context.molecular_weight(role) for the selected component's molecular weight; context.parameters for the immutable parameter values. Return a symbolic reaction-extent rate in mol/(m³ bed·s) . The engine multiplies it by stoichiometric coefficients to form species sources. Do not return a species-consumption rate without accounting for that coefficient. State variables model.c_gas and model.c_sol are per total bed volume. If your rate law uses concentration per gas volume, convert using model.gasfrac . Rate orders come from your equation, independently of stoichiometry. reversible is descriptive metadata; the rate function must implement any reverse or equilibrium term. Document guards for negative trial concentrations and zero inventories. Use the case's resolved properties rather than a global property registry. See Lesson 3 for a complete factory and rate function, and the equations guide for the balance and energy conventions."}, {"url": "plugins.html#dependencies-and-resources", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Dependencies and resources", "text": "Dependencies and resources Keep a plugin self-contained. Include any custom species and correlations its mechanisms need. The three supplied examples work independently; one plugin cannot rely on another plugin's implementation files. Use relative imports between included modules, such as from .helpers import rate_factor . Locate local data relative to __file__ . Supported libraries are Python's standard library, numpy , scipy , yaml , pydantic , xarray , matplotlib , daetools , pyUnits , and the public packed_bed.plugins API. Users should not need plugin-specific pip installs or runtime downloads. Packages cannot include symlinks or additional native libraries/runtimes ( .so , .dll , .pyd , .exe , .whl ). Paths must be portable across supported platforms. The package limit is 100 MiB and 5,000 files. Write archives outside the source plugin folder so they do not become part of the package."}, {"url": "plugins.html#case-yaml-and-engine-integration", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Case YAML and engine integration", "text": "Case YAML and engine integration The application writes these mappings for you. For hand-authored inputs, this chemistry.yaml fragment selects the supplied ammonia example: gas_species: [N2, H2, NH3] species_definitions: NH3: example_ammonia:NH3 reaction_families: [ammonia] mechanisms: ammonia: definition: example_ammonia:synthesis reaction_ids: [ammonia/synthesis] Use my_ammonia:NH3 and my_ammonia:synthesis for the tutorial's own plugin. These are chemistry selections, not a complete reactor case. Supply the other case files and make the plugin available through the project or a descriptor. Program composition maps use case component IDs. When the role and component names differ, add a bindings map inside that mechanism selection, for example bindings: {N2: N2_variant, H2: H2, NH3: NH3} with N2_variant selected in gas_species . Solid components are selected in solids.yaml ; their definition references also belong in chemistry.yaml 's species_definitions map. Reaction IDs normally take the form <instance>/<local-reaction-id> . Existing built-in family instance names retain their legacy reaction IDs, including after a compatible replacement. load_case discovers a containing project and its current plugins. Saved run snapshots use their frozen copies. Standalone inputs can place definitions.json beside run.yaml : { \"root\": \"relative/path/to/project\", \"lock\": { \"builtin\": \"<built-in SHA-256 fingerprint>\", \"plugins\": {\"my_ammonia\": \"<plugin SHA-256 content hash>\"} } } Here root is relative to the descriptor; the selected package is under plugins/my_ammonia/current/ within that root. The strings in angle brackets are placeholders, not valid hashes. Desktop snapshots and CLI batch generation write these descriptors for you. Batch cases use root: \".\" and carry their plugins. An exact lock checks both built-in definitions and selected plugin contents. Mismatches are rejected: use the matching application and plugin copies, or explicitly regenerate inputs against the current definitions. Inputs without an exact lock use current definitions. A descriptor does not grant local Python approval. For Python integration, select_definitions(chemistry, solids, catalogue) and materialize_definitions(selection, catalogue) in packed_bed.definitions (online) separate metadata selection from implementation loading. Materialization returns a DefinitionEnvironment with properties , reaction_network , rate_hooks , and selection . Pass it through load_case(..., definitions=environment) or resolve_case(..., definitions=environment) . Case.definitions is authoritative; a supplied environment does not fill missing definitions from global built-ins. inspect_case and inspect_case_file return metadata-only CaseInputs for editors."}, {"url": "plugins.html#troubleshooting", "page": "Plugin guide", "title": "Troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting What you see What to do Plugin is enabled, but the case still uses the old properties Select the new definition in Chemistry or use Use this as replacement… . No compatible replacement appears Select a species or family entry. Check that an ordinary case or reusable definition uses the old definition and that chemical key/phase or family roles/reaction IDs match. Editing the source files has no effect Register the updated manifest.yaml again; the project holds a copy. A new copy replaces my previous plugin Give it a different top-level id ; changing only name keeps its identity. A parameter is missing from the editor Declare it in Python PARAMETERS , regenerate metadata, then re-register. For existing declared values, edit species parameters or mechanism values . Python declarations or reaction metadata differ from the manifest Run the tutorial build script after editing declarations, then register the generated package. NO is rejected as a species key Quote 'NO' wherever it occurs in YAML. Needs local approval Select the plugin and choose Allow code… on this computer. Missing implementation or unsupported import Include the referenced .py file, use relative imports for your own modules, and use supported libraries. Property check rejects returned values Ensure numerical methods accept arrays and return finite arrays of the same shape. Basic checks sample 300 K and 1000 K; also test your declared range yourself. Missing species or ambiguous roles Add the needed components, then set Bindings… and their compositions/loadings. Remove or Disable is blocked Replace or remove its uses in the cases, reusable definitions, and study baselines listed by the application. Saved input fingerprint or hash mismatch Use the recorded application/plugin contents or explicitly regenerate inputs; do not alter the lock to conceal the mismatch. For a larger scientific package with provenance and fitted kinetics, see the waste iron plugin . The manifest contract is in schema.py (online) , checking and metadata generation in check.py (online) , and packaging in storage.py (online) . Documentation index"}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#cli-guide", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "CLI guide", "text": "CLI guide The command-line interface runs cases and parameter sweeps from YAML files. It writes NetCDF data, a provenance manifest, and any requested plots. Use it from the Python environment described in the installation guide (online) . You can work in any folder containing your input files. The examples below use a folder called my-case ; only the optional repository examples use paths inside a source checkout. Use python from your activated environment. The installed packed-bed command accepts the same arguments as python -m packed_bed . Task Go to Validate or run existing inputs Run a case Create inputs from scratch A complete first case Change a program, bed, or reaction selection Edit the input files Choose a solver or compiled execution Solver settings Generate input diagrams Input diagrams and the DAETools plotter Run parameter combinations Run a batch Read results or export data Outputs and analysis Automate runs from Python Python API Interpret failures Exit codes and troubleshooting"}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#run-a-case", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Run a case", "text": "Run a case A case normally has four YAML files: my-case/ ├── run.yaml ├── chemistry.yaml ├── program.yaml └── solids.yaml run.yaml points to the other three. If someone supplies a case, keep the referenced files in their original relative layout. Validate before running: python -m packed_bed my-case/run.yaml --validate-only Success prints Validation passed: followed by the resolved input path. Then run the same case: python -m packed_bed my-case/run.yaml The terminal shows solver progress, elapsed time, and any requested balance diagnostics. Outputs go to the paths specified in run.yaml . For the complete example below, look in my-case/output/ . A single-case run can replace files at those output paths. Use a separate case folder or change both output directories when you want to keep an earlier result. The CLI does not manage the desktop's run snapshots and status files."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#command-reference", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Command reference", "text": "Command reference python -m packed_bed --help python -m packed_bed batch --help Single-case argument Meaning run.yaml Required positional path to the main case file --validate-only Load and validate inputs without simulating or creating output files --artifacts Generate input diagrams before executing the simulation --dae-plotter Retain the in-memory DAETools process and open its plotter after the run --debug Show a traceback for unexpected errors -h , --help Show command help Simulation settings belong in YAML. There are no single-case --solver , --output , --workers , or --case-timeout-s flags. Batch-specific options are described below ."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#what-validation-checks", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "What validation checks", "text": "What validation checks Validation checks YAML structure, supported fields, species and reaction selections, composition keys and totals, zone coverage, program timing, and report/plot requirements. It resolves the selected scientific definitions. For built-in definitions, validation does not need DAETools or a compiler. It does not prove that a native solver can load, a compiled runtime is available, or a model will integrate successfully. Python plugins require local approval and their factories can execute during CLI validation; --validate-only is not a metadata-only plugin browser. --validate-only returns before --artifacts or --dae-plotter takes effect. It creates no simulation outputs."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#a-complete-first-case", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "A complete first case", "text": "A complete first case Create the folder my-case and save these four UTF-8 files inside it. This is a small nonreacting example: nitrogen flows through an inert alumina bed for 10 seconds. It is intended to demonstrate the input format and workflow."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#1-runyaml", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "1. run.yaml", "text": "1. run.yaml references: chemistry_file: chemistry.yaml program_file: program.yaml solids_file: solids.yaml simulation: system_name: FirstCase time_horizon_s: 10.0 reporting_interval_s: 1.0 repeat_program: false program_mode: separate_channels interior_flow_mode: forward_only mass_scheme: upwind1 heat_scheme: upwind1 report_time_derivatives: false model: bed_length_m: 0.1 bed_radius_m: 0.01 axial_cells: 5 gas_voidage_mode: bed_and_particle ambient_temperature_k: 700.0 heat_transfer_coefficient_w_per_m2_k: 0.0 axial_heat_dispersion_w_per_m_k: 0.0 solver: backend: daetools name: superlu threads: 1 relative_tolerance: 1.0e-6 outputs: directory: output artifacts_directory: output/artifacts requested_reports: - temperature - pressure - gas_mole_fraction - gas_flux - heat_balance - mass_balance requested_plots: - outlet_composition - outlet_conditions"}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#2-chemistryyaml", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "2. chemistry.yaml", "text": "2. chemistry.yaml gas_species: [N2] reaction_families: [] reaction_ids: [] Empty reaction lists are valid for an inert case. A reacting case selects both families and the individual reactions to enable."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#3-solidsyaml", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "3. solids.yaml", "text": "3. solids.yaml solid_species: [Al2O3] initial_profile: basis: bed concentration_unit: mol_per_m3 zones: - x_start_m: 0.0 x_end_m: 0.1 e_b: 0.4 e_p: 0.2 d_p: 0.003 values: Al2O3: 25000.0 Here e_b is interparticle voidage, e_p is intraparticle voidage, and d_p is particle diameter in metres. The solid concentration is per total bed volume because basis is bed ."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#4-programyaml", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "4. program.yaml", "text": "4. program.yaml inlet_flow: basis: mol_per_s initial: 0.001 steps: [] inlet_temperature: initial: 700.0 steps: [] outlet_pressure: initial: 101325.0 steps: [] inlet_composition: initial: {N2: 1.0} steps: [] Empty step lists hold each initial value constant. In hand-authored CLI inputs, the explicit time_horizon_s defines the duration even when all channels are constant. Run the validation and execution commands in Run a case . Checkpoint: a successful run creates results.nc , manifest.json , and the two requested SVG plots. Nitrogen's mole fraction remains one because this case contains a single gas species and no reactions."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#edit-the-input-files", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Edit the input files", "text": "Edit the input files"}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#paths-and-yaml-conventions", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Paths and YAML conventions", "text": "Paths and YAML conventions All paths in run.references , outputs.directory , and outputs.artifacts_directory are relative to the folder containing run.yaml , unless absolute. The artifacts path is not relative to the output directory: use output/artifacts to put it inside output . The shell's working directory only determines how your command finds the initial run.yaml . Use spaces for YAML indentation, ordinary numeric values without unit suffixes, and true / false for booleans. For scientific notation, write 1.0e-7 ; forms such as 1e-7 may be read as strings by the YAML reader. Unknown fields and duplicate mapping keys are rejected. Quote chemical keys such as 'NO' wherever they occur, since unquoted NO is interpreted as a boolean."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#main-run-settings", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Main run settings", "text": "Main run settings Section Main settings references Paths to chemistry, program, and solids files simulation System name, horizon, reporting interval, program mode/repetition, flow mode, mass/heat schemes model Bed length/radius, number of cells, voidage treatment, ambient temperature, wall heat transfer, axial heat dispersion solver Backend, linear solver, thread count, tolerances, and integration controls outputs Output/artifact directories and requested reports/plots Times are in seconds, lengths in metres, temperatures in K, and pressures in Pa. Inlet flows, temperatures, pressures, and step durations must be positive. The reporting interval sets saved samples, separately from the solver's adaptive internal time steps; it must not exceed the simulation horizon. The grid needs at least three axial cells. Supported mass and heat schemes are upwind1 , central , linear_upwind2 , muscl_minmod , weno3 , and weno5 . Set mass and heat schemes independently. See the equations guide for their reconstruction rules and boundary treatment. gas_voidage_mode is bed_only or bed_and_particle . interior_flow_mode is forward_only or reversible ; reversing interior flow does not turn the configured inlet into a negative-flow boundary. heat_transfer_coefficient_w_per_m2_k describes wall heat transfer and axial_heat_dispersion_w_per_m_k describes axial heat spreading. Zero disables the corresponding term. The complete field definitions and defaults are in config/models.py (online) . Write settings explicitly when they matter for reproducibility: omitted YAML settings retain their schema defaults, which differ from the desktop's new-case preset."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#chemistry-and-solids", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Chemistry and solids", "text": "Chemistry and solids gas_species and solid_species identify the case components. Their order also defines the reported species order. Every initial and ramp composition must name exactly the selected gas components, with non-negative fractions summing to one within 1e-12 . Include zero fractions explicitly. Every solid zone must name every selected solid in values . Zones start at zero, meet without gaps or overlaps, and end at the bed length. Changing bed_length_m in YAML does not automatically rescale zone boundaries. Voidages must be strictly between zero and one, particle diameter must be positive, and solid loadings must be non-negative. Solid basis may be bed or solid ; concentration_unit may be mol_per_m3 or kg_per_m3 . Changing either field changes the interpretation of the numbers, not the numbers themselves. The engine converts the chosen basis and units into its internal molar bed-volume concentrations. For reacting cases, reaction_families makes models available and reaction_ids selects the reactions. Include all required species. Custom species references, family instances, and role bindings are documented in the plugin guide . The built-in kinetics sources describe the scientific basis of the supplied reaction families."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#holds-ramps-and-repetition", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Holds, ramps, and repetition", "text": "Holds, ramps, and repetition A hold has kind: hold and positive duration_s . A ramp also has a target . For example, replace the first case's inlet_temperature entry with: inlet_temperature: initial: 700.0 steps: - kind: ramp duration_s: 2.0 target: 750.0 - kind: hold duration_s: 8.0 This defines a temperature change followed by a hold over the 10-second horizon. Ramps use the engine's smoothing; see model equations for the exact time interpolation. With repeat_program: false , every channel that has steps must total the explicit horizon within 1e-9 seconds. A channel with no steps stays constant. With repeat_program: true , schedules repeat and the horizon can span part of a cycle or several cycles. Each channel repeats its own step list, carrying its final value into the next cycle. It does not reset to initial : add a ramp back to the starting value when that is part of the intended cycle. inlet_flow.basis is mol_per_s or ghsv_per_h . GHSV uses the total bed volume and reference conditions 273.15 K and 100,000 Pa . Editing the basis in YAML does not convert the entered values for you."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#combined-feed-mode", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Combined feed mode", "text": "Combined feed mode Set simulation.program_mode: feed_stream to specify flow, temperature, and composition in one feed channel. Replace the entire program.yaml with this alternative for the first case: feed_stream: basis: mol_per_s initial: flow: 0.001 temperature: 700.0 composition: {N2: 1.0} steps: - kind: ramp duration_s: 2.0 target: temperature: 750.0 - kind: hold duration_s: 8.0 outlet_pressure: initial: 101325.0 steps: [] A feed ramp may change flow, temperature, composition, or a combination. Unspecified target fields carry forward their preceding values. The engine interpolates and smooths total molar flow F , species molar flows F*y , and F*T , then divides the latter two by F to obtain composition and temperature. This gives molar-flow-weighted temperature, without a feed enthalpy balance; see Operating programs . Outlet pressure remains separate. Do not mix feed_stream with the independent inlet-channel keys in one program file."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#solver-settings", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Solver settings", "text": "Solver settings Set solver.backend to daetools (the desktop's Standard backend) or compiled . Both use DAETools for model construction and consistent initial conditions; Compiled then integrates the generated native model. Backend Accepted solver.name values daetools superlu , klu , trilinos_lapack , trilinos_aztecoo , trilinos_aztecoo_ifpack , trilinos_aztecoo_ml , sundials_gmres_ifpack compiled superlu , klu , band Availability depends on the installed solver adapters, native libraries, and compiler. Compiled KLU needs the managed runtime with KLU support. See the installation guide (online) and native runtime setup (online) . To try Compiled with the first case, change its existing solver section to: solver: backend: compiled name: band threads: 1 relative_tolerance: 1.0e-6 The first run compiles automatically; compatible later runs reuse the cache. For a standalone case the default is .packed_bed_cache/ beside run.yaml . CLI batches share a cache under their batch output directory. Set PACKED_BED_COMPILED_CACHE to choose another location; an explicit setting takes precedence over both defaults. Relative environment-variable paths are resolved from the process's working directory. Remove a cache only when no run is using it. solver.threads sets numerical threads per case. A positive value sets limits for DAETools and the applicable numerical libraries; 0 keeps environment limits and uses DAETools' default. KLU factorization is serial. Other solver fields include concentration_absolute_tolerance in mol/m³ bed, suppress_algebraic_errors , max_nonlinear_iterations , nonlinear_convergence_coefficient , and maximum_order (1–5). scale_residuals , non-default step_growth_threshold , nonlinear_refresh_interval , and vector_exponentials require Compiled. band_reciprocals additionally requires name: band . Compiled rejects report_time_derivatives: true and outputs.solver_incidence_matrix: true ."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#input-diagrams-and-the-daetools-plotter", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Input diagrams and the DAETools plotter", "text": "Input diagrams and the DAETools plotter To create diagrams and then run: python -m packed_bed my-case/run.yaml --artifacts This writes system_graph.svg , operating_program.svg , and initial_solid_profile.svg in the artifacts directory. The graph needs Graphviz neato , discovered on PATH or through MULTISOLID_GRAPHVIZ . A graph-rendering failure warns and skips that graph while allowing the other diagrams and simulation to continue. To generate only input diagrams, save this as preview_case.py beside my-case and run python preview_case.py : from packed_bed.config import load_case from packed_bed.artifacts import generate_artifacts case = load_case(\"my-case/run.yaml\") for name, path in generate_artifacts(case).items(): print(name, path) This does not integrate the model. Numerical previews do not need DAETools for the built-in definitions. Setting outputs.solver_incidence_matrix: true requests a different artifact: it is generated after solver initialization, requires execution, and is supported only by the Standard backend. To open the DAETools plotter after a run: python -m packed_bed my-case/run.yaml --dae-plotter It needs DAETools' plotter dependencies and a graphical session. Close the window to finish the command. Normal CLI runs and SVG generation do not require opening a GUI. This flag opens the just-completed process, not an arbitrary existing NetCDF file; use Outputs and analysis for saved results."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#run-a-batch", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Run a batch", "text": "Run a batch A batch starts from one base case, applies named variations, and runs every combination. It does not change the base files. For this example, keep the first case in its original separate_channels mode. Save the following as batch.yaml beside the my-case folder: base_case: my-case/run.yaml output_directory: sweep-output workers: 2 case_timeout_s: 120.0 artifacts: false axes: - id: flow values: - id: low patch: program: inlet_flow: initial: 0.001 - id: high patch: program: inlet_flow: initial: 0.002 - id: cells values: - id: coarse patch: run: model: axial_cells: 5 - id: fine patch: run: model: axial_cells: 10 Two flows × two grids produce four cases. Validate them all: python -m packed_bed batch batch.yaml --validate-only Expect Batch validation complete: 4/4 cases passed. . Then execute: python -m packed_bed batch batch.yaml All cases are validated before any generated case files or simulations are created. If one fails validation, the batch stops without starting simulations. After execution starts, individual simulation failures and timeouts are recorded and the remaining cases continue."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#batch-options", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Batch options", "text": "Batch options Argument / YAML field Meaning batch.yaml Required positional path --validate-only Expand and validate every combination without writing outputs --workers N / workers Maximum concurrent cases; positive integer, default 1 --case-timeout-s S / case_timeout_s Positive wall-clock limit per case; default is no timeout --debug Show tracebacks for unexpected errors escaping batch execution YAML artifacts: true Generate input diagrams for each case before execution Command-line workers and timeout override their YAML values: python -m packed_bed batch batch.yaml --workers 2 --case-timeout-s 300 Run that command as an alternative to the preceding execution, or first choose a fresh output_directory . There is no batch --artifacts or --dae-plotter flag. With more than one effective worker, or any timeout, cases run in separate processes. Each retains its own solver.threads setting; workers are capped by the number of cases. With one effective worker and no timeout, cases execute sequentially in the current process. The timeout counts from worker startup and includes diagram generation, compilation, initialization, and output processing, as well as integration. It does not count time spent waiting in the unstarted queue."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#presets-and-patch-order", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Presets and patch order", "text": "Presets and patch order Paths in base_case , output_directory , programs , and geometry solids_file entries are relative to batch.yaml . The base case's referenced input files remain relative to its own run.yaml . In addition to a patch , an axis value can select a named program or geometry . For example, this fragment defines presets: programs: warm_feed: programs/warm.yaml geometries: short_bed: model: bed_length_m: 0.05 solids_file: geometries/short_solids.yaml An axis value can then use program: warm_feed or geometry: short_bed . Supply the referenced files before validating. A program preset replaces the whole program document. A geometry merges its model fields and, if provided, replaces the solids document. Adjust zone boundaries when changing length; CLI batch geometry edits do not rescale them automatically. Axes apply in their listed order. Within one axis value, the order is program, geometry, then patch. Mappings merge recursively; lists and scalar values replace their previous values. For example, a patched steps list replaces the entire list. Later changes win when axes touch the same field. Axis IDs must be unique, and value IDs must be unique within their axis. Every value must specify a program, geometry, or nonempty patch. Each Cartesian combination becomes one case. To list particular pairings instead of a full cross-product, use one axis whose values each carry the complete paired changes."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#batch-outputs-and-reruns", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Batch outputs and reruns", "text": "Batch outputs and reruns The example creates: sweep-output/ ├── summary.csv ├── cases/ │ ├── flow-low__cells-coarse/ │ │ ├── run.yaml │ │ ├── chemistry.yaml │ │ ├── program.yaml │ │ ├── solids.yaml │ │ ├── definitions.json │ │ └── output/ │ │ ├── results.nc │ │ └── manifest.json │ └── ... three more case folders └── .packed_bed_cache/ # Created if compiled execution needs it Case folder names are generated from the axis/value IDs. Each generated run uses local references, a generated system_name , output/ , and output/artifacts/ ; patches cannot override these generated fields. Selected plugin files are copied into the generated case and identified by definitions.json , so those inputs can travel together. Keep the descriptor and any plugins/ folder when moving a generated case. summary.csv records case IDs, axis selections, status, errors, elapsed wall-clock time, paths, plot status/errors, and requested heat/mass balance diagnostics. It is updated during execution. Common statuses are validation_passed , running , success , simulation_failed , timeout_failed , and interrupted_failed . A successful simulation can still have plot errors, recorded separately. Batch execution refuses to overwrite an existing summary or generated case folder. Use a new output_directory for another batch. Validation-only does not check those output collisions. There is no automatic resume command; a generated case can be run separately using its run.yaml , but that does not update the original batch summary."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#outputs-and-analysis", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Outputs and analysis", "text": "Outputs and analysis For the first case, a successful run produces: my-case/output/ ├── results.nc ├── manifest.json └── artifacts/ ├── outlet_composition_vs_time.svg └── outlet_conditions_vs_time.svg manifest.json records resolved configuration and scientific definitions, input/output hashes, software/environment information, runtime and solver statistics, dataset variables/dimensions, balance errors, and plot status. Its schema_version identifies the manifest format (currently 1 ). The application record contains engine and desktop versions, the source commit, whether the source had local changes, and the packaged build identifier. It has its own schema_version for the identity record. A portable desktop reads this identity from its bundled release.json ; Git is not needed on the user's machine. Release wheels retain their build-time source commit. Source runs use the loaded checkout's version and Git state. Unknown values are null ; a Python installation has no packaged build identifier, and an engine-only installation has no UI version. A simulation failure normally attempts to write a failure manifest with a stage and traceback. Early import failures, forced termination, or failures writing the manifest itself may leave none. A failed run may leave samples recorded before failure. Those are reporting-time samples, not rejected solver iterations or necessarily the exact failure instant. Use a fresh output location to avoid confusing old files with a later failed attempt. A single-case run does not clear every unrelated file in its output directory."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#reports-and-plots", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Reports and plots", "text": "Reports and plots Reports select what is saved; plots consume those quantities. An empty requested_plots: [] disables post-run plots. Report ID Main data temperature Inlet, cell, and outlet temperature pressure Inlet, cell, outlet, and pressure drop velocity Superficial velocity at faces gas_concentration Gas species concentration per total bed volume gas_mole_fraction Inlet, cell, and flux-derived outlet composition solid_concentration Solid species concentration per total bed volume solid_mole_fraction Solid mole fractions gas_flux Inlet flow, species face fluxes, and outlet flows reaction_rate Extent rates by reaction and cell; requires selected reactions gas_enthalpy_flux Species enthalpy fluxes at faces heat_balance Stored internal energy, cumulative boundary enthalpy/heat loss, and balance error mass_balance Stored mass, cumulative inlet/outlet mass, and balance error Plot ID Required reports SVG filename outlet_composition gas_mole_fraction outlet_composition_vs_time.svg outlet_conditions temperature , pressure , gas_flux outlet_conditions_vs_time.svg axial_profiles temperature , pressure axial_profiles_vs_time.svg average_solid_composition solid_mole_fraction average_solid_composition_vs_time.svg pca_trajectory All selected reports (at least one) pca_trajectory.svg average_solid_composition averages each solid species' local mole fraction over the uniform bed cells at each reporting time. Empty cells contribute zero. It is a spatial average, not a ratio of the bed's total species inventories. pca_trajectory flattens every numeric, time-dependent report output into one feature per species/location. It centers and standardizes each feature across time, omits constant features, and fits one PCA basis to the saved run. Time and spatial coordinates are excluded. The plot connects one point per reporting time in PC1/PC2 order, colors points by time, marks Start/End, and labels each axis with explained variance. A missing second component is zero; an entirely constant run sits at the origin. Nonfinite report values produce a plot error without dropping samples. Reports with more spatial/species entries contribute more features, and PCA axes are specific to the run and selected reports. Validation rejects missing plot dependencies. Changing a report selection after a run does not add data to the existing NetCDF file. A plot failure leaves the simulation results available, records the error, and makes the CLI return a nonzero exit code."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#read-netcdf-and-export-csv", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Read NetCDF and export CSV", "text": "Read NetCDF and export CSV Save this as inspect_results.py beside my-case , then run python inspect_results.py : import json from pathlib import Path from packed_bed.reports import load_dataset, compute_balance_errors output = Path(\"my-case/output\") dataset = load_dataset(output / \"results.nc\") print(dataset) print(dataset[\"outlet_temperature\"].isel(time=-1).item()) print(compute_balance_errors(dataset)) nitrogen = dataset[\"outlet_composition\"].sel(gas_species=\"N2\") nitrogen.to_series().to_csv(\"nitrogen-outlet.csv\") manifest = json.loads((output / \"manifest.json\").read_text()) print(manifest[\"status\"], manifest[\"plots\"][\"status\"]) The dataset uses named coordinates such as time , x_cell , x_face , gas_species , solid_species , and reaction , where relevant to the requested reports. Physical units are stored in variable attributes. Cell-centre and face positions are distinct grids. Time is stored as elapsed numeric seconds. To recreate a supported plot from saved data without rerunning the solver: from pathlib import Path from packed_bed.reports import load_dataset from packed_bed.plotting import PLOT_REGISTRY dataset = load_dataset(\"my-case/output/results.nc\") plot = PLOT_REGISTRY[\"outlet_composition\"] plot.render(dataset, Path(\"outlet-composition.svg\")) Save this as plot_results.py and run python plot_results.py from the same working folder. The saved data must contain the plot's required variables. There is no CLI Excel-export subcommand. Use Python for your own data processing or the desktop's Report and Results workflows for configured workbooks. Balance diagnostics quantify conservation error; they do not independently establish scientific accuracy or grid convergence."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#plugins-and-desktop-projects", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Plugins and desktop projects", "text": "Plugins and desktop projects The CLI discovers plugins from a containing desktop project, or from a definitions.json beside a standalone/generated run.yaml . Locked inputs must match the recorded built-in fingerprint and plugin hashes. Use the matching application/plugin contents or explicitly regenerate the inputs. Python code needs approval on this computer before loading. Open the project in the desktop application and use Plugins → Enable / Allow code… as appropriate. Copying a project or setting an enabled flag does not convey approval. See the plugin guide for definitions, packaging, and exact locks. CLI batch.yaml files and desktop parameter studies have different workflows. The desktop can import an individual run.yaml , but does not import a CLI batch as a new study. Running desktop-owned input files directly through the CLI also does not update the desktop's run status or snapshots; use the desktop's Run action to keep those records together."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#python-api", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Python API", "text": "Python API For a script that loads and runs a case: from packed_bed.config import load_case from packed_bed.simulation import run_case case = load_case(\"my-case/run.yaml\") result = run_case(case) print(result.status) print(result.results_path) print(result.manifest_path) print(result.plot_errors) Save as run_case.py beside my-case and run python run_case.py . It writes the same configured outputs as the CLI. A nonempty result.plot_errors indicates post-processing failure even when result.status is \"success\" . load_case resolves executable scientific definitions. For metadata-only input inspection without importing plugin implementations, use packed_bed.config.load.inspect_case_file ; its CaseInputs result is for inspection and is not an executable Case . packed_bed.batch.run_batch_file(path, validate_only=True) validates a batch and returns its records. Without validate_only , it executes and writes outputs; workers= and case_timeout_s= provide overrides. Put process-based batch execution inside if __name__ == \"__main__\": in your script, so spawned workers can import it without starting the batch again."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#repository-examples", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Repository examples", "text": "Repository examples With a source checkout, these commands run from the repository root: python -m packed_bed examples/simulations/default_case/run.yaml --validate-only python -m packed_bed batch examples/simulations/default_batch_case/batch.yaml --validate-only The single-case example (online) demonstrates reacting chemistry and timed operating channels. Remove --validate-only to execute it. The batch example (online) combines two programs, two bed geometries, and three solver configurations, for 12 cases . The solver axis includes Standard SuperLU, Compiled SuperLU, and Compiled Band; executing every combination needs their runtimes. Keep only the solver axis values you intend to use. The example has workers: 4 and a much longer simulation horizon than the small first case in this guide."}, {"url": "cli-guide.html#exit-codes-and-troubleshooting", "page": "CLI guide", "title": "Exit codes and troubleshooting", "text": "Exit codes and troubleshooting Exit code Meaning 0 Validation passed, or all simulations and requested plots completed successfully 2 Invalid command syntax, caught input/batch validation error, or a batch refused because of output collisions 1 Other caught execution/setup errors, failed/timed-out batch cases, or requested plot failures These are the normal CLI return codes. Interrupts and external termination may produce platform-specific statuses. --debug re-raises unexpected top-level exceptions; ordinary validation errors remain concise. Batch per-case failures are captured in the summary, so --debug does not turn every worker failure into a terminal traceback. For a terminal log, redirect both standard output and standard error: python -m packed_bed my-case/run.yaml --debug > run.log 2>&1 That command executes the case. run.log is placed in the shell's current directory and overwritten by this redirection. The CLI does not automatically create the desktop's worker.log . Problem What to check A referenced YAML file cannot be found Resolve its path from the folder containing run.yaml ; batch preset paths start at batch.yaml . A number is rejected as a string Use plain numbers with no unit suffix and scientific notation such as 1.0e-7 . Unknown field or duplicate key Check spelling and nesting. Configuration is strict; there are no ignored extra fields. Species/composition mismatch Include every selected gas in every composition and every selected solid in every zone. Program duration mismatch Match each non-empty channel to the explicit horizon for non-repeating programs. Zone coverage fails after a length change Update zone boundaries or supply a matching geometry solids file. Validation passes but execution cannot start Check DAETools, the selected adapter, and the compiled runtime/compiler if used. Validation does not load all native dependencies. Plugin approval or fingerprint mismatch Follow the plugin guide ; preserve the selected package files and descriptor. Batch outputs already exist Choose a fresh batch output directory. Validation-only does not check collisions. A case times out while compiling The timeout includes compilation and initialization; choose a limit that allows them. Results exist but the command returns 1 Inspect plot errors and manifest.json ; post-processing can fail after successful integration. Final batch summary cannot be written Close readers locking summary.csv and check write access. Completed case outputs remain in their directories. No data after a forced stop A worker may have stopped before publishing samples or a manifest. Inspect terminal logs and avoid treating old files as the new result. The implementation references are CLI arguments (online) , configuration models (online) , batch expansion/execution (online) , and reports and provenance (online) . Documentation index"}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#create-your-first-plugin", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "Create your first plugin", "text": "Create your first plugin This walkthrough builds plugins from small, complete files. Start with Lesson 1 if you only need new property coefficients. Continue to Lessons 2 and 3 when you need to write an equation. Lesson What you create Files you write 1. A species using built-in equations Your own nitrogen properties One YAML file 2. A new property equation NH3 with a custom heat-capacity/enthalpy equation A manifest and one Python module 3. A reaction N2 + 3 H2 → 2 NH3 with an editable rate coefficient One more Python module and a short manifest entry Each lesson ends with something you can register in the application. The coefficients and rate law are illustrative, not validated property datasets or an industrial ammonia model. General usage, units, API details, and troubleshooting are in the plugin guide ."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#before-you-start", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "Before you start", "text": "Before you start For Lesson 1, you need a text editor and the desktop application. Alternatively, you can create a species entirely in the application using Create plugin / Add species . For Lessons 2 and 3, use the source installation (online) and activate its Python environment. Run the terminal commands below from the repository root, the folder containing pyproject.toml . The supplied factories keep DAETools imports inside symbolic methods, so generating metadata and running their numerical checks does not require loading the solver. Simulations do. The paths below use a workspace called build/plugin-workshop . For Lesson 1, you can use any writable folder instead; no source checkout is needed. For Lessons 2 and 3, create that workspace inside the repository so the terminal commands match. Use your file manager or editor to create the folders and files. build/ is ignored by Git, so keep a separate copy of work you want to retain or share. Save files as UTF-8 plain text with their exact extensions: manifest.yaml , not manifest.yaml.txt ."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#lesson-1-create-a-plugin-with-one-file", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "Lesson 1: create a plugin with one file", "text": "Lesson 1: create a plugin with one file"}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#1-create-the-folder-and-manifest", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "1. Create the folder and manifest", "text": "1. Create the folder and manifest Inside build/plugin-workshop , create a folder called my_nitrogen . Create my_nitrogen/manifest.yaml with this complete content: id: my_nitrogen name: My nitrogen properties source: Tutorial example; illustrative Cp coefficients. species: N2: name: Nitrogen chemical_key: N2 phase: gas mw: 0.0280134 temperature_range: [298.15, 1200.0] enthalpy: model: builtin:polynomial parameters: coefficients: [30.0, 0.004, 0.000001] t_ref: 298.15 h_form_ref: 0.0 viscosity: model: builtin:quadratic_viscosity parameters: t_ref: 1000.0 a0: 4.15082118e-5 a1: 2.78501267e-8 a2: -6.09391431e-12 That is the entire plugin. The molecular weight and viscosity are taken from the built-in nitrogen definition; the heat-capacity coefficients are illustrative. The fields you are most likely to change are: Field Purpose id: my_nitrogen Identifies this plugin. Use a new ID for a separate plugin, and keep it for later updates. name The label shown in the plugin browser. species: N2: The species' local ID; its full reference is my_nitrogen:N2 . chemical_key: N2 Identifies this as nitrogen, so it can fill an N2 reaction role or replace built-in nitrogen. mw Molecular weight in kg/mol . enthalpy.parameters This species' property coefficients, reference temperature, and reference enthalpy. source Where the values came from; replace the tutorial note with your scientific source for real data. Here Cp = 30 + 0.004 (T − 298.15) + 0.000001 (T − 298.15)² , in J/(mol·K). The application integrates Cp to obtain molar enthalpy, with h(298.15 K) = 0 . The declared temperature interval is in K. Gas species also need viscosity; solid species omit the viscosity entry."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#2-register-it-and-use-it", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "2. Register it and use it", "text": "2. Register it and use it Open a project and choose Plugins → Register plugin… . Select build/plugin-workshop/my_nitrogen/manifest.yaml . Confirm that My nitrogen properties appears as Enabled . Expand it and select Nitrogen to inspect your definition. To change an existing case, select this new nitrogen and choose Use this as replacement… , choose the existing nitrogen definition, and review the affected cases. If no case uses nitrogen yet, add your nitrogen from the species picker in a case's Chemistry tab. Checkpoint: the case's nitrogen definition comes from My nitrogen properties . Merely registering the plugin does not select it in a case."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#3-edit-and-share-it", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "3. Edit and share it", "text": "3. Edit and share it Try changing 30.0 to 31.0 in the coefficients. Register the same manifest again and accept replacement of the project's copy. The top-level id stays my_nitrogen . You can also double-click the plugin species in the application and edit its coefficients there. To share the current project copy, select the plugin and choose Export… . This produces a .msplugin archive that another user can register. You can stop here if built-in property equations meet your needs. The supplied nitrogen oxides manifest (online) follows this same pattern, adding NO and NO2 alongside the N2 variant. Quote 'NO' in YAML: unquoted NO is read as a boolean."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#prepare-the-python-authoring-tools", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "Prepare the Python authoring tools", "text": "Prepare the Python authoring tools The remaining lessons define equations in Python. You will write their parameters and reactions once in Python and let the application library generate the manifest's browsing metadata. Save the following complete script as build/plugin-workshop/build_plugin.py , outside the plugin folders: import argparse from pathlib import Path from packed_bed.plugins.check import check_package, generate_metadata from packed_bed.plugins.storage import package_hash, pack_plugin parser = argparse.ArgumentParser( description=\"Generate metadata, check, and package a plugin you are authoring.\" ) parser.add_argument(\"folder\", type=Path, help=\"Folder containing manifest.yaml\") parser.add_argument(\"archive\", type=Path, help=\"Output .msplugin path outside that folder\") args = parser.parse_args() generate_metadata(args.folder) # Execute this particular local package for the authoring check. # This does not save desktop approval or grant approval to recipients. check_package(args.folder, approved=(package_hash(args.folder),)) archive = pack_plugin(args.folder, args.archive) print(\"Checks passed.\") print(f\"Created {archive}\") This is a helper you create for the tutorial, not a preinstalled command. It executes the package's Python factories, rewrites manifest.yaml with their declarations, checks the package, and creates the archive. Run it on your own authoring files. The first argument is a folder , not its manifest file. The same script also packages Lesson 1: python build/plugin-workshop/build_plugin.py build/plugin-workshop/my_nitrogen dist/plugin-workshop/my_nitrogen.msplugin It should print Checks passed. and the archive path. The output directory is created automatically. Keep the source folder for editing; the archive is what you distribute."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#lesson-2-write-a-new-property-equation", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "Lesson 2: write a new property equation", "text": "Lesson 2: write a new property equation Now create NH3 with a heat capacity that approaches a high-temperature limit: Cp(T) = cp_inf − deficit (t_ref/T)² h(T) = h_ref + cp_inf (T − t_ref) + deficit t_ref² (1/T − 1/t_ref) The second equation is the exact integral of the first, anchored at h_ref . This is the same model used in the supplied ammonia example (online) ."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#1-describe-the-species-and-point-to-the-equation", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "1. Describe the species and point to the equation", "text": "1. Describe the species and point to the equation Create build/plugin-workshop/my_ammonia/manifest.yaml : id: my_ammonia name: My ammonia model source: Tutorial example; properties and kinetics are illustrative. species: NH3: name: Ammonia chemical_key: NH3 phase: gas mw: 0.01703052 temperature_range: [298.15, 1200.0] enthalpy: model: inverse_square parameters: cp_inf: 70.0 deficit: 35.0 t_ref: 298.15 h_ref: -46000.0 viscosity: model: builtin:quadratic_viscosity parameters: t_ref: 298.15 a0: 1.0e-5 a1: 3.0e-8 a2: 0.0 correlations: inverse_square: name: Inverse-square Cp kind: enthalpy implementation: enthalpy:create The connection is: NH3.enthalpy.model: inverse_square → correlations.inverse_square → enthalpy:create → create(parameters) in enthalpy.py enthalpy.parameters supplies values for NH3. The correlation's declarations (defaults, units, descriptions, and bounds) will be generated from Python. Finish the next two steps before registering this manifest."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#2-write-the-equation", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "2. Write the equation", "text": "2. Write the equation Create build/plugin-workshop/my_ammonia/enthalpy.py with this complete content: from dataclasses import dataclass import numpy as np from packed_bed.plugins import BaseCorrelation, ParameterSpec PARAMETERS = { \"cp_inf\": ParameterSpec(70.0, \"J/(mol*K)\", \"High-temperature Cp\", minimum=1), \"deficit\": ParameterSpec(35.0, \"J/(mol*K)\", \"Cp deficit at Tref\", minimum=0), \"t_ref\": ParameterSpec(298.15, \"K\", \"Reference temperature\", minimum=1), \"h_ref\": ParameterSpec(-46000.0, \"J/mol\", \"Enthalpy at Tref\"), } @dataclass(frozen=True) class InverseSquareEnthalpy(BaseCorrelation): cp_inf: float deficit: float t_ref: float h_ref: float def _cp(self, t): return self.cp_inf - self.deficit * (self.t_ref / t)**2 def _h(self, t): return ( self.h_ref + self.cp_inf * (t - self.t_ref) + self.deficit * self.t_ref**2 * (1 / t - 1 / self.t_ref) ) def value(self, temperature): return self._h(np.asarray(temperature, dtype=float)) def cp_value(self, temperature): return self._cp(np.asarray(temperature, dtype=float)) def dae_expression(self, temperature): from daetools.pyDAE import Constant from pyUnits import J, K, mol return self._h(temperature / Constant(1 * K)) * Constant(1 * J / mol) def cp_dae_expression(self, temperature): from daetools.pyDAE import Constant from pyUnits import J, K, mol return self._cp(temperature / Constant(1 * K)) * Constant(1 * J / (mol * K)) def create(parameters): if parameters[\"deficit\"] >= parameters[\"cp_inf\"]: raise ValueError(\"Cp at Tref must be positive: deficit must be smaller than cp_inf.\") return InverseSquareEnthalpy(**parameters) There are three jobs here: PARAMETERS declares the values users may edit, including their units and bounds. The class evaluates the same equations numerically and symbolically. Numerical methods use NumPy and accept arrays. The solver methods normalize temperature to K and attach the output units. create(parameters) checks the relationship between two parameters and returns the correlation object. The application supplies this dictionary. When adapting the example, begin with _cp , its integral _h , and the parameter declarations. Keep numerical and symbolic results consistent. More complex expressions may need separate numerical and DAETools functions; the logarithmic example (online) shows np.log and DAETools Log ."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#3-generate-check-and-package-it", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "3. Generate, check, and package it", "text": "3. Generate, check, and package it Run this from the repository root in your active Python environment: python build/plugin-workshop/build_plugin.py build/plugin-workshop/my_ammonia dist/plugin-workshop/my_ammonia.msplugin Checkpoint: the command prints Checks passed. and creates dist/plugin-workshop/my_ammonia.msplugin . Open the source manifest: the correlations.inverse_square.parameters declarations have been filled in. You keep editing the equation and declarations in Python; regenerate instead of manually synchronizing those generated entries. Register the completed manifest or archive. Select My ammonia model , choose Enable , allow your code, and wait for Checks passed. Its Ammonia species is now available in Chemistry. Double-click the species in Plugins to see the custom correlation's editable parameters."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#4-check-that-the-equation-behaves-as-intended", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "4. Check that the equation behaves as intended", "text": "4. Check that the equation behaves as intended A package check verifies the software contract. Also check your equation. Save this as build/plugin-workshop/check_enthalpy.py , beside my_ammonia : import numpy as np from my_ammonia.enthalpy import PARAMETERS, create parameters = {key: spec.default for key, spec in PARAMETERS.items()} correlation = create(parameters) temperature = np.linspace(parameters[\"t_ref\"], 1200.0, 100) enthalpy = correlation.value(temperature) cp = correlation.cp_value(temperature) assert enthalpy.shape == temperature.shape assert cp.shape == temperature.shape assert np.all(np.isfinite(enthalpy)) assert np.all(np.isfinite(cp)) and np.all(cp > 0) np.testing.assert_allclose( correlation.value(parameters[\"t_ref\"]), parameters[\"h_ref\"] ) step = 0.01 numerical_derivative = ( correlation.value(temperature + step) - correlation.value(temperature - step) ) / (2 * step) np.testing.assert_allclose(numerical_derivative, cp, rtol=1e-7) print(\"Enthalpy reference, array output, positive Cp, and dh/dT checks passed.\") Run: python build/plugin-workshop/check_enthalpy.py This checks the default parameters. Also test any different species parameter sets you supply. Before using a new correlation scientifically, compare the symbolic implementation with the numerical one and verify it against its source data. This small numerical check does not test the solver."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#lesson-3-add-a-reaction", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "Lesson 3: add a reaction", "text": "Lesson 3: add a reaction Keep the my_ammonia folder from Lesson 2. Add a simple reaction: N2 + 3 H2 → 2 NH3 r = k c_N2 c_H2 Concentrations are in mol/m³ bed , and r is in mol/(m³ bed·s). The model is irreversible, first order in each reactant, and has no catalyst, temperature dependence, or equilibrium term."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#1-write-the-rate-and-reaction-definition", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "1. Write the rate and reaction definition", "text": "1. Write the rate and reaction definition Create build/plugin-workshop/my_ammonia/kinetics.py : from packed_bed.plugins import ParameterSpec, ReactionDefinition, ReactionFamily PARAMETERS = { \"k\": ParameterSpec( 0.001, \"m^3 bed/(mol*s)\", \"Rate coefficient using concentrations per bed volume\", minimum=0, ), } def rate(context): from daetools.pyDAE import Constant, Max from pyUnits import m, mol, s model, cell = context.model, context.idx_cell zero = Constant(0 * mol / m**3) nitrogen = Max(model.c_gas(context.gas_index(\"N2\"), cell), zero) hydrogen = Max(model.c_gas(context.gas_index(\"H2\"), cell), zero) return Constant(context.parameters[\"k\"] * m**3 / (mol * s)) * nitrogen * hydrogen def create(parameters): reaction = ReactionDefinition( id=\"synthesis\", name=\"N2 + 3 H2 → 2 NH3\", phase=\"gas_gas\", stoichiometry={\"N2\": -1, \"H2\": -3, \"NH3\": 2}, required_species=(\"N2\", \"H2\", \"NH3\"), source_reference=\"Illustrative plugin example\", notes=\"Irreversible, first order in each reactant. No catalyst, equilibrium or pressure fit.\", ) return ReactionFamily( name=\"Ammonia synthesis\", reactions=(reaction,), required_gas_species=(\"N2\", \"H2\", \"NH3\"), required_solid_species=(), kinetics_hooks={\"synthesis\": rate}, ) You define the chemistry in ReactionDefinition and the equation in rate . ReactionFamily joins them: the \"synthesis\" key in kinetics_hooks must match the reaction's id . The factory must accept parameters ; this rate reads its values later through context.parameters . The index helpers resolve roles to whichever components the case has bound to N2 and H2. Max protects against negative trial concentrations during solving. The engine multiplies your extent rate by stoichiometry: the species sources are −r for N2, −3r for H2, and +2r for NH3. The coefficient −3 does not make the rate third order in H2; a different order must be written in the rate equation."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#2-add-its-factory-to-the-manifest", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "2. Add its factory to the manifest", "text": "2. Add its factory to the manifest Append this at the top level of my_ammonia/manifest.yaml , aligned with species: and correlations: : mechanisms: synthesis: name: Ammonia synthesis implementation: kinetics:create Do not copy the reaction's stoichiometry or parameter declarations into YAML. Run the same build command again: python build/plugin-workshop/build_plugin.py build/plugin-workshop/my_ammonia dist/plugin-workshop/my_ammonia.msplugin The generated manifest now includes the reaction, gas roles, and k declaration. Your complete source folder is: build/plugin-workshop/ ├── build_plugin.py ├── check_enthalpy.py ├── my_nitrogen/ │ └── manifest.yaml └── my_ammonia/ ├── manifest.yaml ├── enthalpy.py └── kinetics.py"}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#3-select-the-reaction-in-a-case", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "3. Select the reaction in a case", "text": "3. Select the reaction in a case Register the updated manifest or archive and confirm replacement of the project's copy. Adding kinetics.py changes the code, so allow it when prompted. In the case's Chemistry tab: Select built-in N2 and H2, and Ammonia from My ammonia model . Choose + Add reaction families… and add Ammonia synthesis from your plugin. Select its N2 + 3 H2 → 2 NH3 reaction and inspect Bindings… . Supply the case's gas compositions and solid-bed configuration. For example, use an inert solid bed and a feed of 0.25 N2 / 0.75 H2 / 0 NH3, within the example's 298.15–1200 K temperature interval. Complete the other reactor and operating inputs before running. Checkpoint: the family and reaction appear in Chemistry, with NH3 bound to your plugin species. This lesson defines the plugin; the feed suggestion is not a complete reactor case."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#4-change-a-rate-parameter-without-rewriting-code", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "4. Change a rate parameter without rewriting code", "text": "4. Change a rate parameter without rewriting code In Plugins , select your synthesis reaction and choose Edit… . Change k from 0.001 to 0.002 , then save. At the same concentrations the rate doubles. To make that change in your source files instead, add values to the existing mechanisms.synthesis entry: values: k: 0.002 Keep the generated fields already in that entry. Run the build command and re-register. values.k overrides the default for this plugin; the default declaration remains 0.001 . Changing the value needs no new code approval. Changing Python parameter names, defaults, units, bounds, or reaction declarations requires metadata regeneration and re-registration. To create a separate model, copy the folder and change its top-level id and name . To add another reaction to the same family, create another ReactionDefinition , add it to reactions , and add its rate function to kinetics_hooks . Include every species the rate reads in the reaction's required_species and the family's gas/solid roles."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#use-the-supplied-examples-as-templates", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "Use the supplied examples as templates", "text": "Use the supplied examples as templates The repository also contains complete, ready-to-register packages: Folder What it demonstrates Files to start with nitrogen_oxides (online) Several species using built-in equations, including an N2 variant manifest.yaml (online) ammonia (online) The custom property equation and rate law built in Lessons 2–3 manifest.yaml (online) , enthalpy.py (online) , kinetics.py (online) enthalpy_overrides (online) Two species sharing a custom correlation with different parameter values manifest.yaml (online) , correlation.py (online) Each folder is independent. You may register its manifest directly; the Python examples need Enable and local code approval. Registering them does not automatically replace any case definitions. For the CO2/H2O example: Copy enthalpy_overrides into your workspace and give its manifest a new id and name if you want an independent plugin. Read correlation.py . It uses Cp = cp_inf − deficit t_ref/T and h = h_ref + cp_inf (T − t_ref) − deficit t_ref log(T/t_ref) . Edit the CO2 or H2O enthalpy.parameters in the manifest. Each species has its own coefficients despite using the same logarithmic correlation. Build with the helper script, register, and enable it. Select the plugin's Carbon Dioxide or Water , then Use this as replacement… to apply it to existing compatible case definitions. Those species retain the built-in molecular weights, viscosities, and reference enthalpies; the Cp curves are illustrative replacements. Their shared code does not require installing a separate correlation plugin."}, {"url": "plugin-examples.html#share-your-finished-example", "page": "Create your first plugin", "title": "Share your finished example", "text": "Share your finished example If you edited source files, share the archive created by build_plugin.py . If you edited the project copy in the application, use Export… from Plugins to share those edits. These are separate copies, so choose the one you changed. Include a README describing the equations, units, source data, intended range, and known limitations. Recipients register the archive and approve Python code locally. For changes to an existing plugin, keep its id ; for a separate alternative, give it a new one. Plugin guide and troubleshooting · Documentation index"}, {"url": "kinetics.html#bundled-kinetics-families", "page": "Bundled kinetics families", "title": "Bundled kinetics families", "text": "Bundled kinetics families Each module owns one swappable family: its reaction definitions, symbolic DAETools hooks, component requirements, and local regularization constants. Run manifests record selected definitions, their parameter values, and the built-in definition fingerprint. They also retain the application version, source commit, and packaged build identifier. Release builds carry this identity without requiring a Git checkout. There is no separate kinetics version system. See run provenance . These references document provenance. They do not assert that a family is appropriate for a particular material or experiment."}, {"url": "kinetics.html#copper--san-pio-et-al", "page": "Bundled kinetics families", "title": "Copper — San Pio et al.", "text": "Copper — San Pio et al. Source: M. A. San Pio et al., Chemical Engineering Science 175 (2018), 56–71, doi:10.1016/j.ces.2017.09.044 (online) . copper_sio2_san_pio ( copper_sio2.py ) contains the two pseudo-homogeneous CuO → Cu2O → Cu hydrogen-reduction steps and the Cu + ½O2 → CuO oxidation step. SiO2 is treated as an inert support, so the family requires no aluminium or spinel species. copper_al2o3_san_pio ( copper_al2o3.py ) contains those support-independent reductions, Cu → CuO oxidation, and the Al2O3-specific spinel reduction and oxidation steps. Both families use the Cu oxidation rate law r = k(T) c_Cu sqrt(p_O2) with oxygen partial pressure in bar, but the paper fits different Arrhenius parameters: Table 5 gives A = 0.193 s^-1 bar^-1/2 and Ea = 180 J/mol for SiO2, while Table 9 gives A = 0.854 s^-1 bar^-1/2 and Ea = 830 J/mol for Al2O3. The SiO2 oxidation hook copies the Al2O3 rate expression and smooth positive-pressure regularization, using the SiO2-specific parameter defaults. The source states that CuO-to-Cu reduction is independent of the support and extends that base model with CuAl2O4/CuAlO2 chemistry for the Al2O3 carrier. The zero-order hydrogen reductions retain a local smooth gas-availability gate. Both copper families evaluate the smooth positive pressure (p + sqrt(p*p + 4*epsilon*epsilon))/2 , with epsilon = 1e-12 bar , using an algebraically equivalent rationalized form in regularization.py . This preserves the small positive tail and its derivative for negative trial pressures without subtracting nearly equal numbers. The rate laws and regularization scale are unchanged."}, {"url": "kinetics.html#nickel-redox--medrano-form", "page": "Bundled kinetics families", "title": "Nickel redox — Medrano form", "text": "Nickel redox — Medrano form nickel_medrano ( nickel_medrano.py ) follows the Medrano-style form documented in Andrew Wright, Chemical Looping Reactor Modelling – 2D , with context from doi:10.1016/j.apenergy.2015.08.078 (online) . The rational, solver-oriented implementation is canonical; the older duplicate form is not retained. The chemical rate constant includes the total-pressure correction used by Argyris et al., Sustainable Energy & Fuels 6 (2022), 2755–2770, doi:10.1039/D2SE00351A (online) , Eq. (14) and Table 2: k j ( T , P ) = k 0 , j exp ( − E a , j R T ) ( P P r e f ) − q j , P r e f = 1 b a r = 10 5 P a . P is the local absolute total gas pressure , recovered from C_total R T . The reference pressure is fixed at 1 bar. This factor modifies the chemical rate constant inside the shrinking-core resistance expression; the ash diffusivity is unchanged. Gas concentration retains its existing reaction-order dependence. At 1 bar the additional factor is exactly one. Each reaction exposes its own dimensionless, nonnegative exponent."}, {"url": "kinetics.html#nickel-catalysed-reforming", "page": "Bundled kinetics families", "title": "Nickel-catalysed reforming", "text": "Nickel-catalysed reforming reforming_xu_froment ( reforming_xu_froment.py ) implements the three Xu–Froment reactions from doi:10.1002/aic.690350109 (online) . reforming_numaguchi ( reforming_numaguchi.py ) implements the alternative Numaguchi–Kikuchi reforming and water-gas-shift form documented in Andrew Wright's technical report. These are alternative published mechanisms, not compatibility aliases."}, {"url": "kinetics.html#iron-redox--he-et-al", "page": "Bundled kinetics families", "title": "Iron redox — He et al.", "text": "Iron redox — He et al. iron_he ( iron_he.py ) contains the He et al. reduction and oxidation family from Energy Conversion and Management 293 (2023), 117525. Its symbolic runtime expressions and local constants are the canonical implementation. Model equations · Documentation index"}, {"url": "installation.html#install-the-desktop-application", "page": "Install the desktop application", "title": "Install the desktop application", "text": "Install the desktop application Download MultiSolid from GitHub Releases (online) . There are four application downloads: an installer and a portable archive for each platform below. SHA256SUMS.txt contains the download checksums."}, {"url": "installation.html#windows", "page": "Install the desktop application", "title": "Windows", "text": "Windows The Windows distribution targets Windows 11 x64 . Choose one release asset: Asset Installation MultiSolid-<version>-windows-x64-setup.exe Run the installer, then open MultiSolid from the Start menu. Installation is for the current user. MultiSolid-<version>-windows-x64-portable.zip Extract the entire ZIP into a new folder, then run MultiSolid/MultiSolid.exe . Keep its files and subfolders together. Release notes state whether the application and installer are signed. Unsigned builds may display an unknown-publisher or reputation warning. Download from the linked GitHub release and use its checksums to verify the files."}, {"url": "installation.html#linux", "page": "Install the desktop application", "title": "Linux", "text": "Linux The Linux distribution targets Ubuntu 24.04 x86_64 . Choose one release asset: Asset Installation MultiSolid-<version>-linux-x64-setup.deb Run sudo apt install ./MultiSolid-<version>-linux-x64-setup.deb , then open MultiSolid from the applications menu. MultiSolid-<version>-linux-x64-portable.tar.gz Extract the entire archive, then run ./MultiSolid/MultiSolid . Keep its files and subfolders together. The installer adds the multisolid command and installs under /opt/multisolid . Remove it with sudo apt remove multisolid . Other Linux distributions are not qualified by the Ubuntu build."}, {"url": "installation.html#projects-and-updates", "page": "Install the desktop application", "title": "Projects and updates", "text": "Projects and updates The desktop distributions include Python, DAETools, Graphviz, and the compiler and libraries needed for Compiled execution. You do not need to install these separately. GitHub's automatically generated source archives contain source code; choose an installer or portable archive for the ready-to-run application. Create projects in a writable folder outside the application directory. The first Compiled run may take several minutes to build its model; compatible later runs reuse the project's cache. To update a portable installation, extract the new version into a new folder and open your existing project. Follow Getting started to create and run your first case, or use the GUI guide for the full project workflow."}, {"url": "notices.html#third-party-notices", "page": "Third-Party Notices", "title": "Third-Party Notices", "text": "Third-Party Notices This project is licensed under GPL-3.0-only . Third-party packages, installations, generated files, and optional tools retain their own licenses. This file is a practical notice summary, not a replacement for the licenses of those projects."}, {"url": "notices.html#runtime-requirements", "page": "Third-Party Notices", "title": "Runtime Requirements", "text": "Runtime Requirements DAETools: GPLv3. Source installations obtain it separately from the DAETools project or a package source you are licensed to use. The Windows desktop distribution bundles its required runtime and notices. PyQt6 and PyQt6-WebEngine: GPL-3.0-only unless used under Riverbank's commercial license. OpenCS: LGPLv3. Install or obtain separately when needed by the DAETools workflow. NumPy: BSD-3-Clause and other permissive notices in the NumPy distribution. xarray: Apache-2.0. pandas: BSD-3-Clause and bundled permissive third-party notices. It is an xarray dependency and supports conversion of labelled results to tables. SciPy: BSD-3-Clause and bundled permissive third-party notices. matplotlib: matplotlib license and bundled third-party notices. openpyxl and its et_xmlfile dependency: MIT. The desktop uses them to write Excel workbooks. Maratype by Artyom Galinov: bundled desktop display font, freely redistributable with no commercial restrictions. pydantic: MIT. PyYAML: MIT."}, {"url": "notices.html#optional-compiled-solver", "page": "Third-Party Notices", "title": "Optional Compiled Solver", "text": "Optional Compiled Solver Compiled execution is included in the Windows desktop distribution. Source installations use the compiled extra for Python dependencies; a staged native bundle can supply the compiler and solver libraries. filelock 3.20.0: Unlicense. Used to coordinate access to compilation caches. scikit-sundae supplies the SUNDIALS runtime for source Compiled execution without a managed bundle. Its wheel and bundled libraries retain their own notices. Managed desktop builds include a separate native runtime with its own dependency notices under compiled/licenses/ . The native Newton helper adapts SUNDIALS control flow under BSD-3-Clause. The bundled license (online) and notice (online) accompany the source and are included in package distributions. The optional vector exponential uses code extracted from SLEEF 3.9.0, copyright Naoki Shibata and contributors, under the Boost Software License 1.0 (online) ."}, {"url": "notices.html#optional-tools", "page": "Third-Party Notices", "title": "Optional Tools", "text": "Optional Tools Graphviz is invoked as a separate executable; no pygraphviz binding is used. Its applicable licence and all bundled native-library/font notices travel with the staged distribution. tools/bundle_graphviz.py retains these notices and records versions, source metadata and file hashes in graphviz/bundle.json . See Graphviz bundling (online) for Windows staging and the release layout and checks (online) . VTK: BSD-3-Clause."}, {"url": "waste-iron.html#waste-iron-plugin", "page": "Waste iron plugin", "title": "Waste iron plugin", "text": "Waste iron plugin Port of waste_iron_kinetics at 9660e5d497265393f49e5a4c51b6c348650415b5 . This package contains the branch's final kinetics and two solid-property definitions. It uses only the application and its bundled libraries."}, {"url": "waste-iron.html#use-in-a-project", "page": "Waste iron plugin", "title": "Use in a project", "text": "Use in a project Choose Plugins → Register plugin and select this manifest.yaml or waste_iron.msplugin . Enable Waste iron and approve its Python code. Add Waste iron redox from this plugin in Chemistry. Select oxidation, reduction, or both. Both reactions need Fe and Fe2O3, available as built-in species. Oxidation needs O2; reduction needs H2 and H2O. Set their bed loadings and feed composition. Add plugin SiO2 and/or Al2O3 when required for the support. To update existing alumina selections, select this plugin's Aluminium Oxide (alpha) and choose Use this as replacement… , then choose the existing Al2O3 definition. The reactions form a new family, not a replacement for built-in iron_he : the waste model uses direct Fe ↔ Fe2O3 conversion, whereas iron_he includes intermediate oxides. Remove other iron reactions from the case if they should not operate alongside this family; selected reaction rates add. For case YAML, the family selection is: gas_species: [N2, O2, H2, H2O] species_definitions: SiO2: waste_iron:SiO2 Al2O3: waste_iron:Al2O3 reaction_families: [fe_waste] mechanisms: fe_waste: definition: waste_iron:fe_waste reaction_ids: [fe_waste/fe_waste_oxidation, fe_waste/fe_waste_reduction] This snippet assumes solids.yaml selects Fe, Fe2O3, SiO2 and Al2O3. Omit any unused support and its definition mapping. Registering the plugin alone does not change existing species selections or reaction families."}, {"url": "waste-iron.html#preserved-kinetics", "page": "Waste iron plugin", "title": "Preserved kinetics", "text": "Preserved kinetics Original file: packed_bed/kinetics/fe_waste.py . Reaction IDs and stoichiometry are retained: fe_waste_oxidation : Fe + 0.75 O2 → 0.5 Fe2O3 . fe_waste_reduction : Fe2O3 + 3 H2 → 2 Fe + 3 H2O . The branch references Solid-State kinetic modelling (iron waste based catalyst).pdf for the kinetics. This port preserves the implementation through 9660e5d , including its later fitted and numerical changes; it is not a fresh transcription or validation of that PDF. Let F and H be Fe and Fe2O3 concentrations in mol/m³ bed . Gas concentrations are in mol/m³ gas , computed as model.c_gas / model.gasfrac . Both returned reaction-extent rates are in mol/(m³ bed·s). For oxidation, S = H + F/2 , X = H/S , and r = 2 S k (1−X) sqrt(1−psi log(1−X+epsilon)) c_O2/(c_O2+K_O2) . The default k is 0.0073738573592314 s⁻¹, psi is 8, K_O2 is 1 mol/m³ gas and epsilon is 1e-10. Fe consumption is r , O2 consumption is 0.75 r and Fe2O3 production is 0.5 r . For reduction, S = F + 2 H , X = F/S , and r = S/2 · k_eff · rational(1−X) · (1−X)^4 · pade(X) . The normalized two-term rational approximation retains (1.452, 11.849, 1.038, 0.170) ; the Padé numerator retains (0.2476, 67.3172, 43.4567) and denominator (1, 92.6243) . The gas law is k_rxn = 0.1382 exp(−37318/(R T)) [(c_H2+1e-4)^0.7554−(1e-4)^0.7554] , with k_eff = k_rxn/(1+k_rxn/0.0180) . Six parameters are editable in the application's family editor: reduction prefactor, activation energy and transfer limit, plus oxidation rate constant, O2 half-saturation and pore parameter. The fitted powers, rational/Padé coefficients and numerical offsets stay fixed in Python. Unused constants from the old implementation, including its unused O2 transfer limit, are omitted. The port clamps negative trial concentrations and protects the zero-carrier denominator and square root. A cell with no Fe or Fe2O3 therefore returns zero rates. For physical inventories the default rates agree with the branch."}, {"url": "waste-iron.html#property-definitions-and-scope", "page": "Waste iron plugin", "title": "Property definitions and scope", "text": "Property definitions and scope The two fits come from d19a65c , unchanged at the branch tip: SiO2 : new solid, 0.060084 kg/mol, reference enthalpy −910857 J/mol. Al2O3 (alpha) : 0.101961 kg/mol, reference enthalpy −1675692 J/mol; replaces the built-in constant Cp with the branch's quartic fit. Both use the existing builtin:polynomial correlation: Cp coefficients multiply powers of T−298.15 K ; enthalpy is its exact integral from the reference value. The manifests retain the branch's coefficients. Original fit data are under Property_Estimation/enth_hcap_data/{sio2,al2o3-alpha} on that branch. Their temperature spans are recorded in the manifest; no new property fit was made. This plugin supplies the branch's rate laws and properties. Axial heat dispersion and solver settings belong to the engine and are not configured by registering the plugin. The current engine supports axial heat dispersion through model.axial_heat_dispersion_w_per_m_k , or Bed → Heat dispersion (W/m/K) in the desktop. See the energy balance . Reproducing a branch reactor result also requires matching its case inputs and numerical settings."}, {"url": "waste-iron.html#edit-and-share", "page": "Waste iron plugin", "title": "Edit and share", "text": "Edit and share Edit declared values in the app, or species/parameter overrides in your source manifest. For source edits, use the source Python environment (online) and save the tutorial build helper as build/plugin-workshop/build_plugin.py . From the repository root, run: python build/plugin-workshop/build_plugin.py plugins/waste_iron dist/plugins/waste_iron.msplugin This regenerates metadata, checks the package, and creates the archive. Unlike the tutorial's lazy imports, this plugin imports DAETools when its kinetics module loads, so authoring checks also need DAETools installed. Re-register the updated manifest or archive to update a project's copy. Use the app's Export… action to share edits made to the project copy instead. The archive includes this documentation, implementation and manifest; it has no dependencies on other plugins. Plugin guide · Documentation index"}, {"url": "index.html#multisolid-help", "page": "MultiSolid help", "title": "MultiSolid help", "text": "MultiSolid help Find your next step, understand the screen you are using, or explore the model and reference guides. Getting started →"}, {"url": "index.html#screen-help", "page": "MultiSolid help", "title": "Help for your screen", "text": "Help for your screen Press F1 in MultiSolid to open the page for the active screen or case tab. Welcome screen Project screen General tab Chemistry tab Bed tab Program tab Report tab Project Results screen Parameter study screen"}, {"url": "index.html#guides", "page": "MultiSolid help", "title": "Guides and reference", "text": "Guides and reference Install the desktop application — downloads and portable installations. User guide — projects, cases, runs, reports, and studies. Model equations — assumptions, balances, and numerical treatment. Plugin guide and authoring examples . CLI guide — YAML inputs, commands, and Python API. Kinetics sources — scientific references for built-in models. Troubleshooting — common problems and checks."}, {"url": "index.html#plugins", "page": "MultiSolid help", "title": "Plugins", "text": "Plugins Documentation for official scientific plugins. Waste iron plugin These pages and their equations work offline. Links marked “online” open external resources."}];
